window.HLD_MCQS={"foundations":[{"q":"Which of these is a \u003cb>low-level design\u003c/b> concern rather than a high-level design concern?","options":["Choosing between a message queue and synchronous calls between two services","Defining the classes and interfaces inside the payment service","Putting a CDN in front of images and other static assets","Splitting user data across database shards by user ID"],"answer":1,"explain":"Classes and interfaces describe the inside of one component, which is LLD. The other three decide which components exist and how data and requests flow between them, which is HLD.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Which of these is a \u003cb>non-functional\u003c/b> requirement?","options":["Users can upload and crop a profile photo","Users can search posts by hashtag or username","Admins can suspend an account for 30 days","The home feed loads in under 200 ms at p99"],"answer":3,"explain":"A latency target describes how well the system works, not what it does. The other options are features a user can observe, which are functional requirements.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Your app and database run on one server. The app code saturates the CPU, and the database's working set no longer fits in memory. What is the most sensible next step?","options":["Move the database to its own machine with more RAM","Shard the database across 16 nodes by user ID","Deploy a second copy of the stack in another region behind GeoDNS","Put a message queue between the app and the database"],"answer":0,"explain":"The problem is two workloads competing for one machine, so separating them is the cheapest fix and lets you give the database more RAM. Sharding or multi-region solve problems of a much larger scale and add a lot of complexity.","level":"medium"},{"q":"You put a load balancer in front of four app servers. Users now report being logged out at random. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The load balancer uses round robin instead of least connections","The database cannot handle four times as many connections","Each app server keeps user sessions in its own local memory","The app servers have different TLS certificate expiry dates"],"answer":2,"explain":"With in-memory sessions, a request that lands on a different server finds no session. Make the servers stateless by keeping sessions in a shared store like Redis or in signed tokens; sticky sessions are a workaround with their own problems.","level":"medium"},{"q":"In which situation do read replicas help the most?","options":["Writes are 80% of traffic and the primary's disk is nearly full","The application needs transactions that span several shards","Reads are 95% of traffic and the primary's CPU is saturated","Every user must always read their own writes immediately"],"answer":2,"explain":"Replicas add read capacity by serving queries from copies. They don't add write capacity or disk space (each holds the full data), and asynchronous replicas can lag, which works against read-your-writes.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Which problem does \u003cb>sharding\u003c/b> solve that adding read replicas does not?","options":["Write throughput and total data size beyond one machine","Slow reads of a few viral posts that everyone requests","Large image files using most of the outbound bandwidth","High latency for users far from the only data center"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every replica stores the full dataset and all writes still go through one primary, so replicas can't help when writes or data outgrow a machine. Sharding splits both. Popular items are a caching problem, images are a CDN problem, and distant users need a CDN or more regions.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A user changes their display name. The page reloads and shows the old name; a second later it shows the new one. The app writes to a primary and reads from asynchronous read replicas, with no cache. What is the cause?","options":["Edge caching: the CDN served a stale copy of the profile page","Load balancing: the reload went to a different app server","Write retry: the primary lost the write and retried it a second later","Replication lag: the read hit a replica that was still behind"],"answer":3,"explain":"Asynchronous replicas trail the primary by milliseconds to seconds. A common fix is to read a user's own data from the primary for a short window after they write. A stateless app tier makes the choice of app server irrelevant.","level":"medium"},{"q":"What does moving images and video delivery to a CDN mainly achieve?","options":["Stronger consistency for profile data read from many regions","Lower latency for distant users and less origin bandwidth","Faster SQL joins by running queries at edge locations","Automatic sharding of the metadata database by region"],"answer":1,"explain":"A CDN caches static files at edge locations near users, so downloads are faster and your origin serves only cache misses. It does nothing for database consistency or partitioning.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Which piece of work is the best candidate to move out of the request path into a queue and background workers?","options":["Generating thumbnails after a user uploads a photo","Verifying a user's password hash during login","Reading the price to show on the checkout page","Checking that a new username is not already taken"],"answer":0,"explain":"Thumbnails can be produced a few seconds later without hurting the user, so a queue makes the upload fast and absorbs spikes. The others are needed to build the response the user is waiting for.","level":"medium"},{"q":"The prompt is “Design an expense-report tool for 2,000 employees of one company.” A candidate proposes three active-active regions, Kafka and 32 Cassandra shards. What is the most likely feedback?","options":["Strong, because it already handles a hundredfold growth in usage","Weak, but only because Cassandra lacks multi-row transactions","Weak, because one region and a relational DB fit this scale","Strong, because multiple regions are required for any 99.9% target"],"answer":2,"explain":"2,000 users generate a trivial load, so the extra complexity costs money and operations for no benefit. Matching the design to the numbers is a core signal, and 99.9% is achievable within one region using multiple availability zones.","level":"hard"},{"q":"In a 45-minute interview, you're at minute 25 and still refining requirements and estimates. What is the best move?","options":["Keep refining, since precise estimates are the most important output","Ask the interviewer to choose the database so you can save time","Skip the API and go straight to a deep dive on caching","State your assumptions and quickly sketch the end-to-end design"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without a working end-to-end design there is nothing to deep-dive into, and that is the most common way to fail. Lock in reasonable assumptions, draw the design, then use the remaining time for depth.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Which latency requirement is the most useful to design against?","options":["Average response time under 100 ms across all endpoints","Feed endpoint p99 under 300 ms, measured at the load balancer","Every request completes in under 1 ms, measured at the client","All responses should be as fast as reasonably possible"],"answer":1,"explain":"It names an operation, a percentile, a threshold and where it is measured, so it can be designed for and tested. Averages hide the slow tail, 1 ms for everything is unrealistic once any network hop is involved, and “as fast as possible” can't be verified.","level":"hard"},{"q":"For which feature is eventual consistency \u003cb>least\u003c/b> acceptable?","options":["The like count shown under a viral post","The follower count shown on a public profile","Reserving the last unit of an item at checkout","A contact's “last seen” timestamp in a chat app"],"answer":2,"explain":"If two buyers can both reserve the last unit, you oversell, which costs money and trust. A like count, follower count or last-seen time that is a few seconds stale harms nobody.","level":"medium"},{"q":"In a deep dive, the interviewer asks: “What happens if your single Redis cache node fails?” What is the best response?","options":["Explain that misses would flood the DB, then add a replica with failover and request coalescing","Explain that Redis keeps data in memory and rarely crashes, so the risk can be accepted as it is","Switch to Memcached, since Memcached replicates every key to the other nodes in the cluster by default","Store all data only in Redis, so that there is no longer a database behind it that could be overloaded"],"answer":0,"explain":"A good answer states the impact first (every request becomes a DB query, which may overload it), then the mitigations: a replica with automatic failover and protection such as request coalescing. Memcached does not replicate keys by default, and dropping the database trades a cache problem for a durability problem.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Why should you estimate QPS and storage early in a design interview?","options":["Because it shows the interviewer you have memorized latency tables","Because the interviewer needs exact figures for capacity planning","Because the numbers decide whether you need shards or caches","Because estimates replace the need to discuss trade-offs later"],"answer":2,"explain":"Estimates exist to drive decisions: whether one database is enough, how big the cache must be, whether a CDN is required. Rough, order-of-magnitude numbers are enough; precision is not the point.","level":"easy"}],"networking":[{"q":"Which DNS server holds the definitive A record for \u003ccode>www.example.com\u003c/code>?","options":["The recursive resolver run by the user's ISP","A root server such as \u003ccode>a.root-servers.net\u003c/code>","The \u003ccode>.com\u003c/code> TLD server run by Verisign","The authoritative nameserver for \u003ccode>example.com\u003c/code>"],"answer":3,"explain":"The authoritative nameserver holds the domain's records. Root and TLD servers only return referrals, and the recursive resolver merely caches answers it fetched from the authoritative server.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Tomorrow you will move your site to a new IP address. The A record's TTL is 86,400 s. What should you do to minimize the time users hit the old IP?","options":["Lower the TTL to 60 s at least a day ahead, then change the record","Change the record tomorrow and set its TTL to 60 s at the same moment","Change the record tomorrow and ask users to clear their browser caches","Keep the TTL and add the new IP as a second A record next to the old one"],"answer":0,"explain":"Resolvers that cached the record before the change keep it for up to the old TTL, so lowering the TTL at switch time is too late. Lower it at least one old-TTL period in advance so caches already hold the short TTL when you switch.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your GeoDNS sends users in Sydney to your US region. All of them work for one company whose DNS resolver runs at headquarters in Chicago. Why?","options":["GeoDNS uses the browser's language setting, which defaults to en-US","Australian ISPs block DNS answers that point to Asia-Pacific regions","GeoDNS sees the IP of the querying resolver, not of the user","The TTL is too short, so the answer keeps flipping between regions"],"answer":2,"explain":"The authoritative server only sees the resolver's IP, so all users behind the Chicago resolver look like they are in Chicago. EDNS Client Subnet can pass part of the client's address, but only if the resolver supports and sends it.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Which HTTP method is \u003cb>idempotent but not safe\u003c/b>?","options":["\u003ccode>GET\u003c/code>","\u003ccode>POST\u003c/code>","\u003ccode>PUT\u003c/code>","\u003ccode>PATCH\u003c/code>"],"answer":2,"explain":"PUT changes state (not safe), but repeating it leaves the resource in the same final state (idempotent). GET is both safe and idempotent; POST is neither; PATCH isn't guaranteed to be idempotent.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A mobile client sends \u003ccode>POST /payments\u003c/code> and the request times out. The client can't tell whether the charge happened. What makes a retry safe?","options":["Retry with \u003ccode>PUT\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>POST\u003c/code>; the server then treats it as idempotent","Send an idempotency key; the server returns the stored result for a repeat","Raise the client timeout to 60 s so the response nearly always arrives","Retry immediately three times; the database rejects exact duplicate rows"],"answer":1,"explain":"With an idempotency key, the server recognizes a repeat of the same logical operation and returns the original result instead of charging twice. Switching the verb doesn't change server behavior, longer timeouts only reduce the problem, and two separate charges aren't duplicate rows.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A logged-in user requests another customer's private invoice, which they have no permission to see. Which status code fits best?","options":["403 Forbidden","401 Unauthorized","400 Bad Request","409 Conflict"],"answer":0,"explain":"403 means the caller is authenticated but not allowed. 401 means not authenticated (missing or invalid credentials). Some APIs return 404 instead to avoid revealing that the invoice exists.","level":"easy"},{"q":"You moved a mobile API from HTTP/1.1 to HTTP/2. On lossy cellular networks, a single lost packet still delays many concurrent requests. What addresses this?","options":["Turning on HTTP/2 server push so responses go out before requests","Opening six HTTP/2 connections per host instead of one","Lowering the DNS TTL so clients reconnect to a closer server","Moving to HTTP/3, whose QUIC streams handle loss independently"],"answer":3,"explain":"HTTP/2 removes HTTP-level blocking, but all its streams share one TCP byte stream, so one loss stalls them all. QUIC delivers each stream independently over UDP, so a loss only delays the streams it carried. Extra connections only partly dilute the problem, and push and DNS are unrelated.","level":"medium"},{"q":"The RTT between a user and your server is 80 ms. Ignoring DNS and server processing, how long until the first response byte arrives on a \u003cb>new\u003c/b> HTTPS connection using TCP and TLS 1.3?","options":["About 80 ms","About 240 ms","About 160 ms","About 320 ms"],"answer":1,"explain":"TCP handshake (1 RTT) + TLS 1.3 handshake (1 RTT) + the request and response (1 RTT) = 3 × 80 = 240 ms. TLS 1.2 adds another round trip, giving 320 ms.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Which workload is the best fit for UDP rather than TCP?","options":["Uploading a 2 GB backup file to object storage","Replicating database writes to a standby","Sending live audio in a voice call","Serving a JSON REST API to mobile apps"],"answer":2,"explain":"In a voice call, audio that arrives late is useless, so retransmission and in-order delivery only add delay; UDP lets the app skip lost samples. The other workloads need every byte delivered correctly and in order.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A browser dashboard shows live stock prices. Data flows only from server to client, it must pass through corporate HTTP proxies, and it should reconnect automatically. What fits best?","options":["WebRTC data channels between the browser and the server","Server-Sent Events over a long-lived HTTP response","Short polling a REST endpoint every 100 ms","A raw TCP socket opened from JavaScript"],"answer":1,"explain":"SSE is one-way server-to-client streaming over ordinary HTTP, and EventSource reconnects automatically with \u003ccode>Last-Event-ID\u003c/code>. WebRTC is for peer media, 100 ms polling is wasteful, and browsers cannot open raw TCP sockets.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your long-polling endpoint holds requests for up to 120 s. Behind a cloud load balancer, many requests fail with 504 at exactly 60 s. What is the most likely fix?","options":["Switch from long polling to short polling every 120 s","Add more app servers so each one holds fewer requests","Lower the DNS TTL so clients find a healthier server","Return each held request after about 25–30 s, even if empty"],"answer":3,"explain":"The load balancer closes connections idle for longer than its timeout (60 s by default on AWS ALB) and returns 504. Long-poll timeouts should stay comfortably below every proxy timeout on the path; raising the LB timeout also works.","level":"hard"},{"q":"You run 2 million WebSocket connections across 20 gateway servers. A deploy restarts all gateways at once, and the authentication service falls over. What is the best fix?","options":["Roll the restart gradually and have clients reconnect with jittered backoff","Switch clients to short polling every 10 s, which places no load on authentication","Raise the heartbeat interval from 30 s to 5 minutes to cut reconnects","Add a second load balancer to split the reconnect burst in half"],"answer":0,"explain":"Restarting everything at once causes 2 million simultaneous reconnects, each authenticating. Rolling restarts and jittered backoff spread reconnections over time. Polling would load auth on every request, heartbeats don't affect reconnect bursts, and a second load balancer still forwards every reconnect to auth.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A company wants all employee web traffic to go through one point that blocks malware sites and logs requests. What is this?","options":["A reverse proxy","A forward proxy","A CDN edge server","An authoritative DNS server"],"answer":1,"explain":"A forward proxy acts on behalf of clients making outbound requests, so it can filter and log them. A reverse proxy and a CDN act on behalf of servers receiving inbound traffic.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Nginx proxies 5,000 requests/s to a single backend IP and port, opening a new connection per request. It starts failing with “cannot assign requested address”. Why, and what fixes it?","options":["The backend ran out of worker threads; add two more backend servers","Nginx's DNS cache is full; increase the resolver cache size","The TLS session cache is full; disable TLS on the upstream hop","TIME_WAIT sockets exhaust ephemeral ports; use upstream keep-alive"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each closed connection holds its ephemeral port in TIME_WAIT for about 60 s: 5,000 × 60 = 300,000 needed, far above the ~28,000 default ports toward one destination. Upstream keep-alive pooling removes the churn.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Dozens of internal services written in Go, Java and Python call each other at 200,000 requests/s in total, with tight latency budgets and some streaming calls. Which API style fits best?","options":["gRPC with Protocol Buffers over HTTP/2","GraphQL with one gateway endpoint per service","REST with JSON over HTTP/1.1 and no connection reuse","SOAP with XML schemas over HTTP/1.1"],"answer":0,"explain":"gRPC gives compact binary encoding, generated typed clients in every language, multiplexed HTTP/2 connections, deadlines and streaming. GraphQL targets flexible client queries, and JSON over HTTP/1.1 without reuse adds parsing and handshake costs.","level":"medium"}],"estimation":[{"q":"A service receives 1 billion requests per day, spread evenly. What is its average request rate?","options":["~1,200 per second","~12,000 per second","~120,000 per second","~1.2 million per second"],"answer":1,"explain":"10^9 ÷ 86,400 ≈ 11,600, about 12,000 per second. Dividing by 10^5 gives 10,000, which is close enough for design decisions.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Roughly how many bytes per second can a 10 Gbps network link carry at most?","options":["~1.25 GB/s","~10 GB/s","~125 MB/s","~80 GB/s"],"answer":0,"explain":"Network speeds are in bits: 10 × 10^9 bits ÷ 8 = 1.25 × 10^9 bytes per second. 10 GB/s confuses bits with bytes, and 125 MB/s is what a 1 Gbps link carries.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Which operation is the \u003cb>slowest\u003c/b>?","options":["A main memory reference","A random 4 KB read from an NVMe SSD","A seek on a spinning hard disk","A round trip between two servers in the same data center"],"answer":2,"explain":"Roughly: memory ~100 ns, SSD random read ~20–100 µs, same-data-center round trip ~0.5 ms, disk seek ~10 ms. The seek is about 20x slower than a local network round trip.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Your SLA promises 99.9% availability per 30-day month. About how much downtime does that allow each month?","options":["~4.3 minutes","~8.8 hours","~7.2 hours","~43 minutes"],"answer":3,"explain":"A 30-day month has 43,200 minutes, and 0.1% of that is 43.2 minutes. 8.8 hours is the yearly allowance at 99.9%, 4.3 minutes is the monthly allowance at 99.99%, and 7.2 hours is the monthly allowance at 99%.","level":"medium"},{"q":"An API serves 4,000 requests per second with an average latency of 250 ms. On average, how many requests are in flight at once?","options":["16","250","1,000","16,000"],"answer":2,"explain":"Little's Law: L = λ × W = 4,000/s × 0.25 s = 1,000. 16 and 16,000 come from dividing instead of multiplying, and 250 is just the latency in milliseconds.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A blocking server has a pool of 400 worker threads. It handles 2,000 requests/s at 100 ms average latency. A database slowdown raises average latency to 300 ms while traffic stays the same. What happens?","options":["Requests in flight rise from ~200 to ~600, beyond 400 threads, so requests queue and latency climbs further","Nothing changes, because the arrival rate is still 2,000 requests per second","Throughput stays at 2,000 requests/s because the extra latency is in the database, not in the threads","Requests in flight fall to ~67, because each request now holds its thread for longer"],"answer":0,"explain":"By Little's Law, in-flight work goes from 2,000 × 0.1 = 200 to 2,000 × 0.3 = 600. The pool can complete at most 400 ÷ 0.3 s ≈ 1,333 requests/s, so a queue builds and keeps growing. This is why timeouts and bounded pools matter.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Users upload 10 million photos a day, about 2 MB each including resized copies. Roughly how much new storage is needed per year, before replication?","options":["~730 TB","~20 TB","~73 PB","~7.3 PB"],"answer":3,"explain":"10^7 × 2 × 10^6 B = 2 × 10^13 B = 20 TB per day; × 365 ≈ 7,300 TB = 7.3 PB per year. 20 TB is only one day's worth.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A news site serves 1 million distinct articles per day, and each is cached as a 20 KB rendered fragment. Using the 80/20 rule, how much memory does the hot set need before overhead?","options":["~400 MB","~4 GB","~20 GB","~200 GB"],"answer":1,"explain":"20% × 1,000,000 × 20 KB = 200,000 × 20 KB = 4 GB. 20 GB would be every distinct article. Even with ~1.5x Redis overhead, one node holds the hot set easily.","level":"medium"},{"q":"You stream video at 5 Mbps to 20,000 concurrent viewers. How much egress bandwidth do you need?","options":["~10 Gbps","~1 Gbps","~100 Gbps","~800 Gbps"],"answer":2,"explain":"20,000 × 5 Mbps = 100,000 Mbps = 100 Gbps, which is 12.5 GB/s. 800 Gbps comes from wrongly multiplying by 8 when the rate was already in bits.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Peak traffic is 30,000 requests/s. One server handles 1,000 requests/s at your target CPU. Servers are spread evenly over 3 availability zones, and the service must carry peak with one whole zone down. How many servers do you need?","options":["30","33","45","90"],"answer":2,"explain":"After losing a zone, two-thirds of the fleet must still provide 30 servers' worth of capacity: 30 ÷ (2/3) = 45. 33 only covers a few individual server failures, and 90 provisions three times the need.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A service averages 10 ms per request when nearly idle. Modeled as a simple single-server queue with random arrivals, roughly what is its average response time at 90% utilization?","options":["~11 ms","~100 ms","~19 ms","~1 s"],"answer":1,"explain":"Response time ≈ service time ÷ (1 − utilization) = 10 ms ÷ 0.1 = 100 ms. Queueing delay grows sharply near saturation, which is why capacity plans target about 50–70% utilization.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A table stores user IDs as a signed 32-bit integer. About how many IDs can it hand out before overflowing?","options":["~65,000","~4.3 billion","~9.2 × 10^18","~2.1 billion"],"answer":3,"explain":"The maximum signed 32-bit value is 2^31 − 1 = 2,147,483,647. 4.3 billion is the unsigned 32-bit range, and 9.2 × 10^18 is the signed 64-bit maximum, which is why IDs should be 64-bit.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your estimates show reads outnumber writes 200 to 1. Which investments pay off first?","options":["Caching, read replicas and a CDN","Write-optimized LSM storage and batched inserts","Partitioning writes across more database primaries","Moving every write onto an asynchronous queue"],"answer":0,"explain":"With reads dominating, serving them from caches, replicas and edge servers removes most of the load. The other options optimize writes, which are only 0.5% of the traffic here.","level":"easy"},{"q":"You must store 100 TB of logical data with replication factor 3, and disks may never be filled beyond 60%. How much raw disk capacity must you provision?","options":["~500 TB","~300 TB","~180 TB","~160 TB"],"answer":0,"explain":"Replicas make 100 × 3 = 300 TB of data; keeping disks at most 60% full needs 300 ÷ 0.6 = 500 TB. 180 TB comes from multiplying by 0.6 instead of dividing.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A food-delivery app operating in one city gets 2 million orders a day, and 40% of them arrive during the 2-hour dinner window. What is the peak order rate?","options":["~23 per second","~111 per second","~56 per second","~280 per second"],"answer":1,"explain":"0.4 × 2,000,000 = 800,000 orders in 7,200 s ≈ 111 per second. The daily average is only ~23 per second, so this regional app peaks at almost 5x its average.","level":"medium"}],"scaling-load-balancing":[{"q":"Your app stores login sessions in each server's memory. After adding a second server behind a round-robin load balancer, users are randomly logged out. What is the most robust fix?","options":["Give each server more RAM so sessions are not evicted early","Move sessions to a shared Redis store keyed by session ID","Switch the load balancer to the least connections algorithm","Shorten the session timeout so stale sessions expire sooner"],"answer":1,"explain":"Round robin sends a user's next request to whichever server is next, and that server has no record of the session. A shared store makes every server see every session; sticky sessions would also hide the symptom but bring uneven load and lost sessions on failure.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Which of these requires a Layer 7 load balancer rather than a Layer 4 one?","options":["Spreading TCP connections evenly across ten servers","Passing the client's source IP address through to backends","Balancing UDP traffic for a DNS service","Sending \u003ccode>/api/*\u003c/code> to one pool and \u003ccode>/images/*\u003c/code> to another"],"answer":3,"explain":"The URL path is inside the HTTP request, which only an L7 balancer parses. L4 balancers handle TCP and UDP distribution and can preserve source IPs (NLB does), but they never see the path.","level":"easy"},{"q":"gRPC clients reach 10 backends through an L4 load balancer. You scale to 20 backends, but the new ones receive almost no traffic. What is the most likely cause?","options":["An L4 balancer picks a backend once per long-lived HTTP/2 connection","The L4 balancer caches its backend list until it is restarted","gRPC requires sticky sessions, so clients stay on their original backends","The new backends fail health checks because gRPC runs over UDP"],"answer":0,"explain":"An L4 balancer chooses a backend once per connection, and gRPC clients reuse one connection for a long time, so existing clients stay pinned. Use an L7 proxy that balances individual HTTP/2 streams (such as Envoy) or client-side balancing. gRPC runs over TCP, not UDP.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your API mixes 10 ms lookups with report exports that take up to 60 seconds. With round robin, some servers pile up slow requests while others sit idle. Which algorithm fits best?","options":["IP hash, so each user's reports always land on the same server","Weighted round robin with higher weights on larger instances","Least connections, so new requests avoid busy servers","Random selection, which averages out over many requests"],"answer":2,"explain":"Least connections routes each new request to the server with the fewest open connections, which steers work away from servers stuck on long exports. Weights and randomness are blind to current load, and IP hash would concentrate heavy users.","level":"medium"},{"q":"What does connection draining (deregistration delay) do when an instance is removed from a load balancer?","options":["Immediately closes every open connection so clients reconnect elsewhere","Stops new requests but lets in-flight requests finish, up to a timeout","Moves the instance's open TCP connections to another healthy instance","Delays the health check so the instance is not marked unhealthy too early"],"answer":1,"explain":"Draining stops routing new traffic but gives existing requests time to complete, so deploys and scale-in drop nothing. TCP connections cannot be migrated between ordinary servers; they finish or get closed gracefully.","level":"easy"},{"q":"You balance with IP hash. One server is overloaded while others are quiet, and its traffic comes mostly from a single large corporate customer. Why?","options":["IP hash weights servers by request size, and that customer sends large requests","The corporate firewall blocks the other servers, so traffic retries onto one","Hash collisions are common when IP addresses are close together","The customer's staff share one public IP behind NAT, so all hash alike"],"answer":3,"explain":"IP hash maps each source IP to one server. Corporate NAT and carrier-grade NAT put many users behind a single IP, so they all land on the same backend. Cookie-based affinity or a stateless design avoids this.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Ten cache-backed servers are chosen with \u003ccode>hash(key) mod N\u003c/code>. You add an eleventh and the hit rate collapses. Roughly what fraction of keys would move if you used consistent hashing instead?","options":["About 50%","About 91%","About 9%","Exactly 0%"],"answer":2,"explain":"With consistent hashing, a new node takes over only its share of the ring, about 1/11 (9%) of keys. With mod N, going from 10 to 11 remaps about 10/11 (91%) of keys, which is why every cache went cold at once.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Compliance requires traffic to be encrypted all the way to the application servers, and you also need routing by URL path. Which setup meets both?","options":["Terminate TLS at an L7 balancer, route by path, and re-encrypt","Use TLS passthrough at an L4 balancer and route by URL path","Terminate TLS at the balancer and forward plain HTTP inside the VPC","Route by SNI hostname at an L4 balancer without decrypting"],"answer":0,"explain":"Path routing needs the decrypted HTTP request, and re-encrypting (TLS bridging, often mTLS) keeps every hop encrypted. Passthrough and SNI routing never see the path, and forwarding plain HTTP breaks the compliance rule.","level":"medium"},{"q":"In a keepalived (VRRP) active-passive pair, the standby node takes over the virtual IP. How does the local network learn that the VIP now belongs to a different machine?","options":["The DNS record for the VIP expires and resolvers fetch the new address","BGP withdraws the route from the failed node within a few seconds","Clients detect the failure and retry against the standby's own IP","The new master sends gratuitous ARP so switches map the VIP to its MAC"],"answer":3,"explain":"The VIP's IP address does not change, only the machine behind it. Gratuitous ARP announces the new IP-to-MAC mapping so switches redirect traffic immediately. DNS and BGP operate at other scopes and are not involved in a same-subnet VRRP failover.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Why do many global services put an anycast IP in front of their regions instead of relying only on DNS-based failover?","options":["Anycast encrypts all traffic between the user and the nearest region","BGP routes users to a nearby healthy site, with no wait for DNS caches","Anycast pins each TCP connection to one server for its lifetime","Anycast removes the need for any load balancers inside each region"],"answer":1,"explain":"With anycast, every site announces the same IP; a failed site withdraws its route and traffic shifts as BGP converges. DNS failover depends on resolvers honouring TTLs, and many clients keep stale answers for minutes. You still need regional load balancers behind the anycast edge.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Each instance's \u003ccode>/health\u003c/code> endpoint queries the shared PostgreSQL database. During a 15-second database failover, what is the most likely outcome?","options":["Only the instances currently running queries are marked unhealthy","The LB keeps routing normally because health checks run once a minute","Every instance fails the check, so the LB may pull the whole fleet","The LB automatically routes traffic to the database replica instead"],"answer":2,"explain":"A shared dependency in the health check makes all instances fail together, turning a partial problem into a total one. Keep LB readiness checks local to the instance and handle dependency failures with timeouts and circuit breakers. Some LBs fail open (ALB, Envoy's panic threshold) precisely to limit this.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A Kubernetes HPA targets 60% average CPU. There are 8 pods averaging 90% CPU. How many replicas will it request?","options":["10","12","14","16"],"answer":1,"explain":"HPA computes ceil(current x actual / target) = ceil(8 x 90 / 60) = ceil(12) = 12. At 12 pods the same total load averages about 60% per pod.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Your Auto Scaling group adds instances when CPU is above 70% and removes them when CPU is below 60%. It oscillates every few minutes. What is the best fix?","options":["Widen the threshold gap and add a longer scale-in stabilization window","Scale on memory instead of CPU, since memory usage changes more slowly","Lower the scale-out threshold to 50% so instances are added earlier","Disable scale-in completely and only ever scale out"],"answer":0,"explain":"Removing one instance pushes the others above 70% because the thresholds are too close, and the scaler acts before metrics settle. A wider band plus a slow, stabilized scale-in stops the flapping. Disabling scale-in works but wastes money, and a 50% scale-out threshold would overlap the 60% scale-in one.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A flash sale starts at 12:00. Traffic rises 20x within 60 seconds, and new instances need about 4 minutes to become ready. What best keeps the site up?","options":["Lower the scale-out CPU threshold from 70% to 30% before the sale","Shorten the cooldown so the group can scale every 30 seconds","Pre-scale with a scheduled action before noon, plus a warm pool","Use step scaling that adds 100% capacity when CPU passes 80%"],"answer":2,"explain":"Any reactive policy first has to detect the spike and then wait about 4 minutes for instances, so users are overloaded for most of a short sale. For a known event, provision capacity ahead of time and keep a warm pool for overflow; reactive policies then fine-tune.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Which is a fundamental limitation of vertical scaling?","options":["It requires rewriting the service to keep no local state","It needs a load balancer in front of the bigger server","It cannot be used for databases that need ACID transactions","There is a size ceiling, and the one machine is still a SPOF"],"answer":3,"explain":"Scaling up needs no code changes or load balancer, and it is common for databases, but you eventually hit the biggest machine available and still have one box that can fail. Horizontal scaling removes both limits at the cost of complexity.","level":"easy"}],"caching":[{"q":"A Redis lookup takes 1 ms and a database query takes 20 ms. On a miss the app checks Redis and then queries the DB. With a 95% hit ratio, what is the average read latency?","options":["About 1.05 ms","About 2 ms","About 5 ms","About 10.5 ms"],"answer":1,"explain":"Average = 0.95 x 1 + 0.05 x (1 + 20) = 0.95 + 1.05 = 2.0 ms. The tempting 1.05 ms forgets that each miss also pays the cache lookup and the full DB query.","level":"medium"},{"q":"At constant traffic, you improve the cache hit ratio from 90% to 99%. What happens to the load on the database?","options":["It drops by about 9%","It roughly halves","It drops about 10x","It drops about 100x"],"answer":2,"explain":"Database load follows the miss ratio, which goes from 10% to 1%, a 10x reduction. This is why small improvements in an already high hit ratio matter so much for capacity.","level":"easy"},{"q":"In the cache-aside pattern, what happens on a read miss?","options":["The application queries the database, then stores the result in the cache","The cache loads the value from the database itself, on the application's behalf","The database pushes the row into the cache through a trigger on the table","The application returns an error and a background job fills the cache later"],"answer":0,"explain":"In cache-aside the application owns both steps: read the DB on a miss and populate the cache. When the cache does the loading itself, that is read-through.","level":"easy"},{"q":"With cache-aside, why do teams usually \u003cem>delete\u003c/em> the cache key after updating the database instead of writing the new value into the cache?","options":["Redis replicates a DEL to replicas synchronously but a SET only asynchronously","A SET without an expiry argument makes the key expire at once, so readers never see it","Redis refuses to overwrite a key that still has a TTL until that TTL runs out","Racing writers can update the cache in the wrong order and leave a stale value behind"],"answer":3,"explain":"Two writers may commit in one order and update the cache in the other, leaving stale data until the TTL. Deleting makes the next reader load whatever the database holds, regardless of ordering, and avoids caching values nobody reads.","level":"medium"},{"q":"What is the main risk of a write-behind (write-back) cache?","options":["Every read has to go to the database first, so read latency gets worse","Acknowledged writes are lost if the cache fails before it flushes them","Every write waits for both cache and database, doubling write latency","It needs a two-phase commit between the cache and the database"],"answer":1,"explain":"Write-behind acknowledges writes once they are in the cache and flushes later, so a crash loses whatever has not been flushed. Waiting for the database on every write is the cost of write-through, which write-behind avoids.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A service writes millions of audit records per day; they are rarely read, and when they are, it is weeks later. Which write strategy best protects the cache's hit ratio for other data?","options":["Write-through, writing to the cache and the database together","Write-behind, writing to the cache and flushing to the database later","Write-around, writing to the database only and caching on read","Refresh-ahead, reloading cached records before their TTL expires"],"answer":2,"explain":"Write-around keeps write-once, rarely read data out of the cache, so it does not evict hot entries. Write-through would flood the cache with records nobody reads, and write-behind risks losing audit data.","level":"medium"},{"q":"An O(1) LRU cache uses a hash map plus a linked list. Why must the list be \u003cem>doubly\u003c/em> linked?","options":["To unlink any node in O(1), which needs a pointer to its predecessor","So eviction can scan from both ends of the list to find the least recent entry","To allow head and tail sentinel nodes, which singly linked lists cannot have","To hold the key and the value in two separate pointers on each node"],"answer":0,"explain":"On every hit the node moves to the front, which means removing it from wherever it is. Without a prev pointer you would walk the list to find the predecessor, making each operation O(n).","level":"hard"},{"q":"Every night a reporting job reads every product once, and each morning the cache hit ratio for real users has collapsed. The cache uses LRU. Which change helps most?","options":["Increase the TTL on product keys so hot entries live longer in the cache","Switch to FIFO, which evicts keys strictly in the order they were inserted","Lower \u003ccode>maxmemory\u003c/code> so the scanned keys are evicted before morning","Switch to LFU or W-TinyLFU, which rank keys by how often they are read"],"answer":3,"explain":"Under LRU, a scan makes every one-off item most recent and evicts the hot set. LFU-style policies count frequency, so items read once cannot displace items read thousands of times. Better still, let the batch job bypass the cache.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A homepage key read 20,000 times per second expires, and the database is hit by thousands of identical rebuild queries at once. Which fix targets this directly?","options":["Put a Bloom filter of valid keys in front of the cache to screen requests","Coalesce requests or take a per-key lock so only one request reloads the value","Add random jitter to every key's TTL so expiries spread out over time","Replicate the key under several names so its reads spread across shards"],"answer":1,"explain":"This is a cache stampede on one key; coalescing or a lock limits rebuilds to one, while other callers wait or get the stale value. Bloom filters address penetration, TTL jitter addresses many keys expiring together (avalanche), and key replication addresses read throughput on a hot key.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A scraper requests random product IDs that do not exist. Each one misses the cache and queries the database. What is the best defence?","options":["Increase the cache size so more product keys stay resident in memory","Switch eviction to LFU so frequently requested IDs are kept longer","Cache not-found results briefly and screen IDs with a Bloom filter","Use write-through so every product is cached as soon as it is written"],"answer":2,"explain":"This is cache penetration. Negative caching turns repeated misses into hits, and a Bloom filter rejects IDs that are definitely absent without touching the database. A bigger cache or a different eviction policy does nothing for keys that never get cached.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A nightly job loads 2 million keys into Redis, all with a TTL of exactly 3,600 seconds. About an hour later the database spikes. What is the simplest fix?","options":["Add random jitter to each TTL so the keys expire at different times","Shorten the TTL to 600 seconds so keys reload more often","Set expiries with \u003ccode>PEXPIRE\u003c/code> so they are precise to the millisecond","Switch Redis to \u003ccode>volatile-ttl\u003c/code> so keys nearest expiry are evicted first"],"answer":0,"explain":"Identical TTLs make all keys expire together, a cache avalanche. Adding jitter (for example plus 0 to 10% of the TTL) spreads the misses over time. A shorter TTL just causes the same spike more often, millisecond precision still expires every key at the same moment, and eviction policies only act under memory pressure.","level":"easy"},{"q":"One product key receives 600,000 reads per second during a flash deal and saturates its Redis Cluster shard. Why does adding more shards not help, and what does?","options":["New shards need hours to migrate slots; move the shard to a larger instance type","The cluster is limited to 16,384 operations per second per slot; raise the slot count","Cluster clients read from replicas by default; disable replica reads on the client","A key maps to one slot on one shard; cache it in process and copy it under several keys"],"answer":3,"explain":"Sharding spreads different keys, not a single key, so one hot key still lands on one node. A short-TTL local cache absorbs most reads, and copies such as product:42:r0 to r9 spread the remainder across shards. 16,384 is the number of slots, not a throughput limit.","level":"hard"},{"q":"In Redis Cluster, a Lua script that updates \u003ccode>user:42:cart\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>user:42:total\u003c/code> fails with a \u003ccode>CROSSSLOT\u003c/code> error. What is the right fix?","options":["Wrap the commands in \u003ccode>MULTI\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>EXEC\u003c/code> instead of running a Lua script","Raise the hash slot count so that both keys can fit in the same slot","Use a hash tag in both names: \u003ccode>{user:42}:cart\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>{user:42}:total\u003c/code>","Run the script with \u003ccode>EVAL\u003c/code> on each primary that owns one of the keys"],"answer":2,"explain":"Multi-key operations in Redis Cluster require all keys in one slot. With a hash tag, only the text inside braces is hashed, so both keys share a slot. MULTI/EXEC has the same same-slot restriction, and the slot count is fixed at 16,384.","level":"hard"},{"q":"After a deploy, some users keep getting the old \u003ccode>app.js\u003c/code> for a day, because it is cached with \u003ccode>max-age=86400\u003c/code>. What is the best long-term practice?","options":["Use content-hashed names like \u003ccode>app.3f9a1c.js\u003c/code>, cached for a year as \u003ccode>immutable\u003c/code>","Serve all JavaScript with \u003ccode>no-store\u003c/code> so browsers always fetch the latest file","Keep the same file name and purge the CDN cache as the last step of every deploy","Lower \u003ccode>max-age\u003c/code> on JavaScript to 60 seconds so old copies expire quickly"],"answer":0,"explain":"A new file name is a new cache key, so every client fetches the new version as soon as the HTML references it, while unchanged files stay cached forever. Purging cannot reach browser caches, and short or no caching throws away performance.","level":"easy"},{"q":"An API response has \u003ccode>Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, s-maxage=600\u003c/code>. How long may the browser and the CDN serve it without revalidating?","options":["Both for 600 seconds, because s-maxage overrides max-age everywhere","Both for 60 seconds; s-maxage only controls how long stale copies may be served","The CDN for 60 seconds and the browser for 600 seconds","The browser for 60 seconds and the CDN for 600 seconds"],"answer":3,"explain":"s-maxage applies only to shared caches and overrides max-age there; private browser caches ignore it and use max-age. This split lets a CDN absorb traffic for longer while browsers check back sooner.","level":"hard"}],"databases":[{"q":"A transfer debits account A and credits account B in one transaction. The server crashes after the debit is written but before COMMIT. Which ACID property guarantees that the debit is undone on restart?","options":["Atomicity","Consistency","Isolation","Durability"],"answer":0,"explain":"Atomicity means all-or-nothing: on recovery the engine uses its log to roll back work that never committed. Durability is the opposite promise, that work which \u003cem>did\u003c/em> commit survives the crash.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Two web requests read \u003ccode>likes = 10\u003c/code> for the same post, each adds 1 in application code, and each writes back \u003ccode>likes = 11\u003c/code>. What anomaly is this?","options":["Dirty read","Phantom read","Lost update","Write skew"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both transactions did a read-modify-write on the same row, and the second write overwrote the first. Write skew is the related case where the two transactions write \u003cem>different\u003c/em> rows. The cheapest fix is an atomic \u003ccode>UPDATE posts SET likes = likes + 1\u003c/code>.","level":"easy"},{"q":"What are the default isolation levels of PostgreSQL and MySQL with InnoDB?","options":["PostgreSQL and InnoDB both default to Repeatable Read","PostgreSQL: Read Committed; InnoDB: Repeatable Read","PostgreSQL: Serializable; InnoDB: Read Committed","PostgreSQL: Repeatable Read; InnoDB: Read Committed"],"answer":1,"explain":"PostgreSQL, like Oracle and SQL Server, defaults to Read Committed; InnoDB defaults to Repeatable Read. The same application code can be safe on one and racy on the other, so always name the database when discussing isolation.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Which database family fits a query like 'find people within 3 hops of me who follow the same artists', computed on demand?","options":["A wide-column store such as Cassandra","A key-value store such as Redis","A time-series database such as InfluxDB","A graph database such as Neo4j"],"answer":3,"explain":"Graph databases store relationships as first-class edges, so a multi-hop traversal costs roughly the size of the neighborhood visited. In relational or wide-column stores each hop is another join or round of lookups, and the cost explodes with depth.","level":"easy"},{"q":"You store \u003ccode>author_name\u003c/code> on every post row to avoid a join on the feed page. What is the main cost you take on?","options":["Each feed read must now lock the author's row in the users table","The posts table loses its primary key, since rows are no longer unique","Posts can no longer be indexed by author once the foreign key is gone","Each rename must update many rows, or the copies drift apart"],"answer":3,"explain":"Denormalization moves cost from reads to writes: one fact now lives in many places, so every change fans out, and you need a mechanism (same transaction, trigger or CDC) to keep the copies in sync. Reads get faster, and the table keeps its primary key and can still be indexed by author.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A table has a composite B-tree index on \u003ccode>(tenant_id, status, created_at)\u003c/code>. Which query \u003cb>cannot\u003c/b> use the index to seek directly to its rows?","options":["\u003ccode>WHERE tenant_id = 7\u003c/code>","\u003ccode>WHERE tenant_id = 7 AND status = 'open' ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20\u003c/code>","\u003ccode>WHERE status = 'open' AND created_at &gt; '2026-01-01'\u003c/code>","\u003ccode>WHERE tenant_id = 7 AND status = 'open'\u003c/code>"],"answer":2,"explain":"The index is sorted by \u003ccode>tenant_id\u003c/code> first, so without a predicate on it there is no contiguous range to seek to (the leftmost-prefix rule). The second option is ideal: two equality columns, then rows already in \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code> order, so there is no sort step.","level":"medium"},{"q":"\u003ccode>SELECT status, total FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code> uses an index on \u003ccode>customer_id\u003c/code>, then reads every matching row from the table and sorts them. What is the best fix?","options":["Replace it with a hash index on \u003ccode>customer_id\u003c/code> for O(1) lookups","Index \u003ccode>(customer_id, created_at)\u003c/code> and include \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>total\u003c/code>","Add single-column indexes on \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>total\u003c/code>","Partition the orders table by \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> so each query scans less"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ordering by \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code> within each customer removes the sort and lets the database stop after 10 entries, and including the selected columns makes the index covering, so it never touches the table. Separate single-column indexes cannot provide that order or that coverage.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A B+tree index uses 8 KB pages with a fan-out of about 500. Roughly how many levels does it need for 100 million rows?","options":["About 3","About 7","About 27","About 100"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each level multiplies capacity by about 500: 500 squared is 250,000 and 500 cubed is 125 million, so three levels are enough. 27 is the depth of a binary tree, which is why databases use wide pages. With the top levels cached, a lookup costs one or two disk reads.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A sensor fleet writes 300k small readings per second, almost never updates them, and queries recent ranges per sensor. Why does an LSM-tree engine such as Cassandra or RocksDB suit this better than a B-tree engine?","options":["LSM-trees update rows in place on disk, so they avoid write amplification","LSM-trees keep each key in exactly one file, so range scans read a single file","LSM-trees make every read a single disk seek regardless of data size","LSM-trees buffer writes in memory and flush sorted files sequentially"],"answer":3,"explain":"An LSM engine appends to a log and a memtable, then writes immutable sorted SSTables sequentially, so it absorbs high write rates without random page updates. The price is read amplification and background compaction; in-place updates describe B-trees, not LSM-trees.","level":"medium"},{"q":"An admin opens a product edit form, works on it for several minutes, then clicks Save. Two admins sometimes edit the same product. Which concurrency control fits best?","options":["Take \u003ccode>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE\u003c/code> when the form opens and release it on Save","Run the save at Read Uncommitted so it never waits on other writers","Check a \u003ccode>version\u003c/code> column in the \u003ccode>UPDATE ... WHERE\u003c/code> clause and reject stale saves","Let the last write win, since the later Save holds the newest data"],"answer":2,"explain":"Optimistic locking suits low contention and long think time: no lock is held while the person edits, and if the conditional update matches no row, the save is rejected as a conflict. Holding a row lock across HTTP requests and minutes of user time blocks other writers and leaks locks when browsers close.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A reporting job on a busy PostgreSQL primary keeps one transaction open for 6 hours. What side effect should you expect on heavily updated tables?","options":["Writers on those tables block until the report's transaction commits","Dead row versions pile up, as VACUUM must keep any the old snapshot may need","Row locks taken by the updaters escalate to table locks until the report ends","The WAL is truncated early, so commits made during the report lose durability"],"answer":1,"explain":"Under MVCC readers do not block writers, but the oldest open snapshot pins every row version it might still need, so VACUUM cannot reclaim them and tables and indexes bloat. PostgreSQL never escalates row locks to table locks. Run long reports on a replica or in short chunks, and set \u003ccode>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout\u003c/code>.","level":"medium"},{"q":"An InnoDB table uses random UUIDv4 primary keys and has 6 secondary indexes. Once the table outgrows the buffer pool, insert throughput collapses. What is the best explanation?","options":["Random keys scatter inserts across the clustered index, causing page splits and cache misses","B-trees cannot order 128-bit UUIDs, so InnoDB checks uniqueness with a full table scan on insert","InnoDB takes a table-level AUTO-INC lock for every UUID insert, serializing the writers","Each secondary index on a UUID-keyed table is rebuilt from scratch after every insert"],"answer":0,"explain":"InnoDB clusters rows by primary key, so random keys land on random pages that must be read into memory and often split, while increasing keys append at the right edge. Every secondary index entry also stores the primary key. Time-ordered IDs such as UUIDv7 or Snowflake (Chapter 14) keep inserts local.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Rule: at least one doctor must be on call. Alice and Bob are both on call. Each runs a transaction at PostgreSQL Repeatable Read that counts on-call doctors (gets 2), then sets their own row to off call. Both commit. What happened, and what prevents it?","options":["A lost update; Repeatable Read's first-updater-wins check already prevents it","A dirty read; switching both transactions to Read Committed prevents it","Write skew; run at Serializable with retries, or read the rows \u003ccode>FOR UPDATE\u003c/code>","A non-repeatable read; re-running the count just before COMMIT prevents it"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each transaction wrote a different row, so snapshot isolation sees no write-write conflict, yet together they break the invariant: that is write skew. PostgreSQL's Serializable (SSI) detects the dependency and aborts one transaction, and \u003ccode>FOR UPDATE\u003c/code> on the on-call rows also serializes them. Re-counting does not help, because the snapshot never changes.","level":"hard"},{"q":"On MySQL InnoDB at Repeatable Read, two transactions each run a plain \u003ccode>SELECT balance\u003c/code> (both see 100), compute 100 - 30 in the app, and run \u003ccode>UPDATE accounts SET balance = 70\u003c/code>. What happens?","options":["The second UPDATE fails with a serialization error, exactly as it would in PostgreSQL","Both commit and one withdrawal is lost; use \u003ccode>FOR UPDATE\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>SET balance = balance - 30\u003c/code>","InnoDB detects a deadlock between the two UPDATEs and rolls one transaction back","The second UPDATE matches zero rows, because its snapshot predates the first commit"],"answer":1,"explain":"InnoDB's UPDATE does a current read of the latest committed row: the second transaction waits for the first's row lock, then overwrites 70 with 70. PostgreSQL Repeatable Read would instead abort the second writer with \u003cem>could not serialize access due to concurrent update\u003c/em>. A locking read or a relative update fixes it.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A Cassandra table using size-tiered compaction gets frequent updates to the same rows, and reads are slow because each one merges data from many SSTables. Which change most directly cuts read amplification, and what does it cost?","options":["Switch to leveled compaction; reads check about one SSTable per level, but write amplification rises","Switch to time-window compaction; it merges all windows into one file, but uses more disk while merging","Shrink the memtable so data flushes sooner and reads hit fewer SSTables, but flushes run more often","Raise \u003ccode>gc_grace_seconds\u003c/code> so reads can skip tombstoned rows, but deleted data stays on disk longer"],"answer":0,"explain":"Leveled compaction keeps SSTables non-overlapping within each level, bounding how many files can hold a key, at the price of higher write amplification and compaction I/O. Time-window compaction never merges across windows and suits append-only TTL data, a smaller memtable creates \u003cem>more\u003c/em> SSTables, and keeping tombstones longer gives reads more to merge, not less.","level":"hard"}],"replication-sharding":[{"q":"A PostgreSQL primary replicates asynchronously to one standby. It acknowledges a commit, crashes before that WAL record reaches the standby, and the standby is promoted. What happens to the write?","options":["It is replayed on the standby automatically from the client's retry buffer","It is lost, even though the client was told it committed","Promotion is blocked until the old primary comes back","The standby refuses promotion because it detects the missing WAL"],"answer":1,"explain":"Asynchronous replication acknowledges after the local commit only, so anything not yet shipped disappears on failover. Synchronous or semi-synchronous replication to at least one standby prevents this at the cost of a round trip per commit.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A user changes their display name, the page reloads, and the old name appears. Reads go to asynchronous replicas. Which guarantee is broken, and what is the cheapest fix?","options":["Monotonic reads; add more read replicas so each one carries less lag","Consistent prefix reads; move every user's rows into a single partition","Linearizability; run every query at Serializable isolation","Read-your-writes; read the user's own profile from the leader"],"answer":3,"explain":"The user is not seeing their own write, which is a read-your-writes violation. Routing only 'my own data' reads to the leader, or to a replica that has replayed past the write's log position, fixes it without slowing everyone else. More replicas would make the lag problem no better.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A cache tier picks a node with \u003ccode>hash(key) % N\u003c/code>. You grow it from 10 to 11 nodes. Roughly what fraction of keys now map to a different node?","options":["About 90%","About 9%","About 50%","Almost none"],"answer":0,"explain":"A key stays put only if its hash modulo 10 equals its hash modulo 11, which holds for about 1 in 11 keys, so about 91% move and the cache hit ratio collapses. About 9% (1/11) is what consistent hashing would move.","level":"easy"},{"q":"An events table is range-partitioned by \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code>. Under steady write load, one node runs at 100% CPU while the others are nearly idle. Why?","options":["Each range holds at most a few million rows, so full ranges reject new rows","Every insert must first lock the partition map, which lives on one node","Each insert carries the newest timestamp, so it lands in the last range","Range partitioning hashes each timestamp, and the hash skews toward one node"],"answer":2,"explain":"With a monotonically increasing key, the newest range receives every insert. Leading the key with something that spreads writes, such as \u003ccode>(source_id, created_at)\u003c/code>, or hashing the key, fixes it at the cost of cheap time-range scans across all sources.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A leaderless store keeps N = 3 replicas per key. Which setting guarantees that reads overlap the latest successful write while still tolerating one replica down for both reads and writes?","options":["W = 1, R = 1","W = 3, R = 1","W = 2, R = 2","W = 1, R = 2"],"answer":2,"explain":"2 + 2 &gt; 3 guarantees overlap, and both reads and writes need only 2 of 3 replicas. W = 3, R = 1 also overlaps, but a single dead replica blocks every write. W = 1, R = 2 sums to 3, which is not greater than N.","level":"medium"},{"q":"With N = 5, W = 3 and R = 3, how many of a key's replicas can be unavailable while both reads and writes still succeed?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"explain":"Writes need 3 of 5 and reads need 3 of 5, so each tolerates 5 - 3 = 2 replicas down, and 3 + 3 = 6 &gt; 5 keeps the overlap guarantee. This is why N = 5 is chosen when two simultaneous failures must be survivable.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A user sees a new comment, refreshes and it is gone, then refreshes again and it is back. Reads are load-balanced randomly across three asynchronous replicas. Which fix targets this specific problem?","options":["Route each user's reads to the same replica by hashing the user ID","Raise the replication factor from 3 to 5 so more replicas hold the comment","Switch from row-based to statement-based replication to cut replica lag","Read at consistency level ONE so the fastest replica always answers"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is a monotonic-reads violation: successive reads hit replicas with different lag, so time appears to go backwards. Sticky routing per user means a user only ever moves forward through one replica's history. More replicas make random routing worse, not better.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A celebrity's post gets 50,000 likes per second. The like counter is a single DynamoDB item, and writes are being throttled. What is the standard fix?","options":["Change the partition key to a hash of the post ID so writes spread out","Raise the table's provisioned write capacity tenfold to absorb the burst","Move the counter into a global secondary index with its own capacity","Split the counter across sub-keys \u003ccode>post42:0\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>post42:99\u003c/code> and sum on read"],"answer":3,"explain":"One item always lives in one partition, which is capped at about 1,000 write units per second; partition keys are already hashed, and table-level capacity does not lift that per-partition cap. Write sharding spreads the key over many partitions, and reads sum the parts or use a cached total.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A products table is hash-partitioned by \u003ccode>product_id\u003c/code> across 50 partitions, and each partition keeps a local secondary index on \u003ccode>color\u003c/code>. What does \u003ccode>WHERE color = 'red'\u003c/code> cost?","options":["One request to the partition that owns the term red","A request to all 50 partitions, with the results merged","A full scan of every partition, because local indexes only serve writes","Two requests: one to a global index, then one to a data partition"],"answer":1,"explain":"A local (document-partitioned) index only covers its own partition's rows, so a query by secondary key must scatter to all partitions and gather the results, and its latency follows the slowest one. Only a global (term-partitioned) index answers from a single index partition.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Compared with one token per node, what is the main benefit of giving each node 200 virtual nodes on a consistent-hashing ring?","options":["Load evens out, and a failed node's keys spread over many nodes, not one","Adding a node no longer moves any existing keys to the new node","Range queries over adjacent keys stay on a single node","Lookups get faster, because the ring no longer needs a hash function"],"answer":0,"explain":"Many small arcs per node average out the randomness of token placement and spread both the extra load and the recovery work when a node leaves. Keys still move when nodes join (about 1/N of them), and hashing still scatters adjacent keys.","level":"medium"},{"q":"During a network partition, a Dynamo-style store accepts writes on healthy nodes outside a key's usual replica set and forwards them to the right nodes after they recover. What does this trade?","options":["Gains consistency, but each write must wait for every replica to acknowledge","Gains lower latency, so read repair and anti-entropy can be switched off","Gains durability, so no write is lost even if a stand-in node dies for good","Gains write availability, but R + W &gt; N no longer ensures a fresh read"],"answer":3,"explain":"A sloppy quorum with hinted handoff keeps writes flowing when home replicas are unreachable, but the W acknowledgments may come from nodes that a read quorum of home replicas never consults, so reads can miss the write until handoff completes. Read repair and anti-entropy are still needed, for example if a stand-in node dies before handing off.","level":"medium"},{"q":"After a network partition, a replica was promoted, but the old primary is still running and some app servers keep writing to it. Which approach most directly prevents two nodes from accepting writes at once?","options":["Shorten the failure-detection timeout so failover completes sooner","Switch to synchronous replication so both nodes always hold identical data","Hold leadership as an etcd lease and reject writes from an older epoch","Merge both primaries' writes with last write wins once the partition heals"],"answer":2,"explain":"A leader on the minority side cannot renew its lease and steps down, and epoch fencing lets replicas and storage refuse the stale leader even if it has not noticed yet. Shorter timeouts make false failovers more likely, and LWW silently discards writes that were acknowledged.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Two regions run multi-leader replication with last write wins. Within 50 ms, a user in us-east sets a document's title to 'Q3 plan' and a user in eu-west sets it to 'Q3 roadmap'. Both get success. What happens, and what would let the system detect the conflict?","options":["One title silently replaces the other everywhere; version vectors would detect it","The later write is rejected with a conflict error; LWW already detects it","Each region keeps its own title until a resync; Lamport clocks would detect it","Both titles are merged into one string; synchronized NTP clocks would detect it"],"answer":0,"explain":"LWW converges by keeping the highest timestamp, so one acknowledged write disappears without any error, and with clock skew it may not even be the later one. A version vector shows that neither write had seen the other, so the system can keep both as siblings or merge them. Lamport clocks and synchronized wall clocks always impose an order, so they cannot reveal concurrency.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A Dynamo-style store runs with N = 3, W = 2, R = 2. In which situation can a read still return a value older than the latest acknowledged write?","options":["All three replicas are healthy, and the write completed before the read started","A sloppy quorum put the write on two stand-in nodes outside the home replicas","One home replica was down during both the write and the read that followed","The client raises R to 3 and reads from all three home replicas"],"answer":1,"explain":"With a sloppy quorum, the two acknowledgments came from stand-in nodes, so a read of two home replicas can miss the write entirely until hinted handoff runs. With one home replica down, the write and the read both use the same two surviving replicas, so they still overlap.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A Cassandra keyspace has replication factor 3 in each of two datacenters, 6 replicas in total. A user writes in DC1 and then immediately reads in DC2. Which pair of consistency levels guarantees that the read sees the write?","options":["Write LOCAL_QUORUM, read LOCAL_QUORUM","Write LOCAL_QUORUM, read QUORUM (4 of 6)","Write EACH_QUORUM, read LOCAL_QUORUM","Write ONE, read LOCAL_QUORUM with read repair"],"answer":2,"explain":"EACH_QUORUM puts the write on 2 of 3 replicas in every datacenter, so a 2-of-3 read in DC2 must overlap it (2 + 2 &gt; 3), and the read stays local. A LOCAL_QUORUM write touches only DC1, and pairing it with a 4-of-6 read gives 2 + 4 = 6, which is not greater than 6, so the read can miss.","level":"hard"}],"distributed-theory":[{"q":"According to the CAP theorem as proved by Gilbert and Lynch, what must a replicated data store give up while a network partition is in progress?","options":["Either linearizable reads and writes, or a non-error reply from every live node","Either durability of committed writes, or tolerance of the partition itself","Either low latency, or high throughput on the majority side of the partition","Either ACID transactions, or secondary indexes that span every partition"],"answer":0,"explain":"CAP's C is linearizability and its A means every non-failing node answers. During a partition you can keep one of the two. It says nothing about durability, throughput or indexes.","level":"easy"},{"q":"An architect says: 'Our 3-node database cluster is CA, because it runs in one data center with redundant switches.' What is the best response?","options":["Agreed: redundant switches rule out partitions, so the cluster can be both C and A","Partitions still occur in one data center; during one, nodes must refuse or risk stale reads","It is CP by default, because every cluster with more than one node is automatically CP","It is AP by default, because a single data center has no cross-region latency to worry about"],"answer":1,"explain":"Redundancy lowers the odds of a partition but cannot rule one out (misconfigurations, NIC faults, GC pauses that look like partitions). P is a given, so the real design decision is behavior during a partition.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Under PACELC, how is Cassandra best classified when clients read and write at consistency level ONE?","options":["PC/EC: it refuses writes during partitions and coordinates on every read","PC/EL: it refuses writes during partitions but answers quickly otherwise","PA/EL: it stays available during partitions and favors latency otherwise","PA/EC: it stays available during partitions but coordinates every read otherwise"],"answer":2,"explain":"At ONE, any single replica can accept a write or answer a read, so Cassandra stays available under partition and avoids coordination latency normally. Raising the level to QUORUM moves it toward consistency at a latency cost.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A database runs serializable transactions on its leader but serves read-only transactions from asynchronous replicas. A user's read-only transaction returns a consistent snapshot from 2 seconds ago. Which statement is accurate?","options":["It violates serializability, because it misses the latest commit","It is allowed by serializability, though the read is not linearizable","It violates every isolation level, because any stale read breaks isolation","It satisfies strict serializability, because the snapshot is internally consistent"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serializability only requires equivalence to some serial order, which may place the read before recent commits. Linearizability (and strict serializability) add the real-time requirement that this read must see writes completed before it began.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Why do teams run consensus clusters with 3 or 5 nodes rather than 4 or 6?","options":["Raft cannot elect a leader when the cluster has an even number of nodes","Even sizes cause a split vote in every election, so no leader ever stabilizes","Odd sizes let the leader skip writing entries to its own log before replicating","An even size raises the majority needed but tolerates no more failures"],"answer":3,"explain":"Four nodes need a majority of three and tolerate one failure, the same as three nodes. Raft works with even sizes; it just gains nothing from the extra node. Randomized timeouts handle split votes regardless of size.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A Raft follower in term 5 receives a RequestVote for term 6 from a candidate whose last log entry has term 4 and index 9. The follower's own last entry has term 5 and index 6. What does the follower do?","options":["Grants the vote and moves to term 6, because the candidate's log is longer","Rejects the vote and stays in term 5, because the candidate's log is behind","Moves to term 6 but rejects the vote, as its own last entry has a higher term","Grants the vote and moves to term 6, because a higher term always wins the vote"],"answer":2,"explain":"Any higher term makes a server adopt it, but the vote also requires the candidate's log to be at least as up to date: last-entry terms are compared first, and 5 beats 4. This rule ensures a leader always holds every committed entry.","level":"hard"},{"q":"In Raft, when may the leader treat a new log entry from its current term as committed and apply it to the state machine?","options":["As soon as it is appended to and flushed in the leader's own log","When every follower in the cluster has acknowledged it","When a majority of servers, including the leader, store it","When the next election timeout passes with no new leader elected"],"answer":2,"explain":"Majority storage guarantees any future leader, which needs votes from a majority and must have an up-to-date log, will contain the entry. Waiting for every follower would let one slow node stall the cluster.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your leaderless key-value store must detect when two clients wrote the same key concurrently, so it can keep both versions as siblings. Which mechanism supports this?","options":["Lamport timestamps attached to each written value","Wall-clock timestamps compared with last-write-wins","A monotonic clock read on each replica at write time","Version vectors attached to each stored value"],"answer":3,"explain":"Comparing vectors element-wise reveals when neither version dominates, which is exactly concurrency. Lamport timestamps and wall clocks always produce an order, so they silently pick a winner instead of detecting the conflict.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Spanner assigns a commit timestamp s and waits until TrueTime guarantees s has passed before acknowledging the commit. What does this commit wait achieve?","options":["It ensures a Paxos majority has persisted the write before the client gets a reply","It ensures any transaction starting after the ack gets a later commit timestamp","It gives the time servers a chance to resynchronize the clocks of all replicas","It ensures no other transaction still holds a lock on any of the same rows"],"answer":1,"explain":"Commit wait converts bounded clock uncertainty into external consistency: timestamp order matches real-time commit order. Paxos replication happens in parallel with the wait; it is not what the wait is for.","level":"hard"},{"q":"In two-phase commit, a participant has voted YES and then loses contact with the coordinator. What can it safely do?","options":["Stay prepared, holding its locks, until it learns the decision","Abort after a timeout, since no commit message has arrived","Commit after a timeout, since it has already voted YES","Ask the client application which outcome it should apply"],"answer":0,"explain":"After a YES vote the participant is in doubt: others may have committed, or the decision may have been ABORT. It must wait to hear the outcome from the coordinator or from a participant that knows it; acting unilaterally could break atomicity, which is exactly why 2PC is called a blocking protocol.","level":"medium"},{"q":"In an order saga, step 1 reserves the last unit of an item and another shopper is told it is sold out. Step 2, the card charge, then fails, and the compensation releases the unit. Which ACID property does a saga not provide that allowed this?","options":["Isolation between concurrent sagas","Atomicity of each local step","Durability of committed steps","Consistency of each service's local constraints"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each saga step commits locally, so intermediate states are visible to everyone. Semantic locks (marking the unit PENDING) and step ordering are the usual countermeasures.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A worker holds a 10-second lease from etcd, then stalls in a 30-second GC pause. Another worker acquires the lease and writes. What actually prevents the first worker's late write from corrupting the file?","options":["The first worker checks that its lease is still valid right before every write","A lease TTL chosen to be longer than the longest GC pause ever observed","The storage service rejects writes whose fencing token is below the highest seen","etcd closes all of the first worker's TCP connections as soon as its lease expires"],"answer":2,"explain":"Only the resource can enforce exclusion after the fact. A client-side check races with the pause, a longer TTL only narrows the window, and etcd cannot stop a client from talking to a different system.","level":"hard"},{"q":"An order service inserts an order into PostgreSQL and then publishes OrderPlaced to Kafka in the same request handler. What is the problem, and what is the standard fix?","options":["Kafka adds latency to the request; publish the event first, then insert the order","A crash between steps leaves them out of sync; insert the event into an outbox table in the same transaction","Kafka rejects raw JSON payloads; encode the event in Avro and register its schema before publishing","A crash between steps leaves them out of sync; give the producer a transactional.id so both commit atomically"],"answer":1,"explain":"This is the dual-write problem. Kafka transactions cover only Kafka partitions and offsets, not a PostgreSQL write, so the outbox puts both changes into one local database transaction and a relay (or CDC such as Debezium) publishes the event afterward.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Your service calls a payment API and the call times out after 2 seconds. What do you know about the payment?","options":["Nothing for certain: it may never have arrived, still be running, or have succeeded","It failed, so it is safe to retry right away with a brand-new request","It succeeded, because a timeout only happens when the response is slow","It was never received, because a timeout means the connection itself failed"],"answer":0,"explain":"A timeout cannot distinguish a lost request from a lost response. Retrying is only safe if the request carries an idempotency key, or after querying the payment's status.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A comments service must never show anyone a reply before the comment it answers, and it must keep accepting writes in every region during a cross-region partition. What is the strongest model you can realistically promise?","options":["Linearizability","Strict serializability","Sequential consistency","Causal consistency"],"answer":3,"explain":"Causal consistency preserves the comment-before-reply order and can be provided while every region stays available. Linearizability, strict serializability and sequential consistency all need cross-region coordination, so they cannot stay available during the partition.","level":"medium"}],"messaging":[{"q":"Checkout calls a third-party email API synchronously to send receipts. When the provider slows to 4-second responses, checkout p99 latency explodes. What is the best fix?","options":["Increase the checkout service's thread pool so more requests can wait in parallel","Cache the email provider's responses in Redis with a 5-minute TTL per customer","Retry the email call up to three times with a 500 ms timeout per attempt","Publish an OrderPlaced event and send the receipt from an async queue consumer"],"answer":3,"explain":"The receipt is not on the critical path, so a queue decouples checkout from the provider's latency and availability. More threads or retries keep checkout coupled to the slow dependency, and caching makes no sense for a side effect.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Shipping receives an \u003ccode>OrderPlaced\u003c/code> event containing only \u003ccode>order_id\u003c/code>, then calls the Orders API to fetch the address and items. Which pattern is this?","options":["Event notification","Event-carried state transfer","Event sourcing","Change data capture"],"answer":0,"explain":"A thin event that tells consumers something happened, with a callback for details, is event notification. Event-carried state transfer would put the address and items inside the event so no callback is needed.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A Kafka consumer processes each record and then commits its offset. It crashes after processing a record but before committing. What happens on restart?","options":["The record is skipped, giving at-most-once semantics","The record is processed again, giving at-least-once semantics","The broker detects the crash and rolls back the record's side effects","The record is moved automatically to the group's dead-letter topic"],"answer":1,"explain":"The new owner resumes from the last committed offset, which is before the processed record, so it is redelivered. That is why at-least-once consumers must be idempotent; Kafka has no built-in DLQ for plain consumers.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A topic has 8 partitions and producers send records without keys. Which ordering guarantee does a consumer have?","options":["Records arrive in the exact global order that producers sent them","Records are ordered by producer timestamp across all partitions","Records are ordered within each partition, but not across partitions","No ordering at all; unkeyed records are shuffled inside a partition"],"answer":2,"explain":"Kafka guarantees order only within a partition; offsets define that order. Without keys, related records spread across partitions, so nothing orders them relative to one another.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A ride-tracking service publishes trip events (requested, accepted, started, completed) and consumers must process each trip's events in order. Most riders take one trip at a time, but a few corporate accounts book thousands of trips a day. What should the partition key be?","options":["account_id, so all of an account's trips are colocated","trip_id, so each trip's events stay in one partition","city, so regional consumers get their data together","No key, so load spreads evenly across partitions"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ordering requirement is per trip, and trip_id has high cardinality with no dominant value. account_id would create hot partitions for corporate accounts, city is badly skewed, and no key loses per-trip ordering.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A consumer group on a 12-partition topic has 12 consumers, and lag keeps growing during peak. You scale the group to 24 consumers. What happens?","options":["Throughput doubles, because Kafka splits each partition between two consumers","Throughput doubles, but records with the same key may be processed out of order","The group rejects the 12 extra consumers with a rebalance error","12 consumers sit idle, so lag keeps growing at the same rate as before"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each partition is assigned to at most one consumer in a group, so 12 partitions cap the group at 12 active consumers. The extras simply wait as standbys for a failure; to go faster you need more partitions or faster per-record processing.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A topic has replication factor 3 and \u003ccode>min.insync.replicas=2\u003c/code>; producers use \u003ccode>acks=all\u003c/code>. Two of a partition's three brokers go down. What happens?","options":["Writes still succeed with acks=all, since the ISR shrinks to just the leader","The partition goes offline for both reads and writes until a second broker returns","Producers get NotEnoughReplicas errors, but consumers can still read committed data","Kafka automatically elects an out-of-sync replica so writes can continue"],"answer":2,"explain":"With the ISR down to one replica, below min.insync.replicas, the leader refuses acks=all writes rather than accept data a single failure could destroy. Committed data remains readable from the surviving leader, and unclean election is off by default.","level":"medium"},{"q":"With \u003ccode>enable.idempotence=true\u003c/code>, which duplicate can still end up in the Kafka log?","options":["The client library retries a batch whose acknowledgment was lost","The app crashes, restarts and re-sends a message it had already sent","A partition leader fails over while a producer batch is still in flight","A network partition makes the producer reconnect to the same broker"],"answer":1,"explain":"Idempotence dedupes retries within one producer session using the producer ID and per-partition sequence numbers. After a restart the producer has a new producer ID and the resend is a new record; preventing that needs transactions or consumer-side dedupe.","level":"medium"},{"q":"One record in a partition has a malformed payload that crashes the consumer's deserializer every time. The consumer retries indefinitely and the partition's lag climbs. What is the right handling?","options":["Send the record and its error to a dead-letter topic, then commit past it","Restart the consumer group so the record is assigned to a different consumer","Lower the topic's retention so the bad record is deleted within minutes","Increase max.poll.interval.ms so the consumer has more time to parse the record"],"answer":0,"explain":"A poison message fails deterministically, so retries, extra time and reassignment change nothing. Parking it in a DLQ (and alerting) unblocks the partition and preserves the payload for a fix and later redrive.","level":"medium"},{"q":"An SQS queue uses the default 30-second visibility timeout, but each job takes about 60 seconds to process. What will you observe, and how do you fix it?","options":["Jobs fail with a timeout error; fix it by enabling 20-second long polling","SQS throttles the slow consumers; fix it by adding more worker instances","Messages are deleted after 30 seconds; fix it by raising the retention period","Jobs run more than once; fix it by raising the visibility timeout past 60 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"When the visibility timeout expires before the worker deletes the message, SQS makes it visible again and another worker picks it up. Set the timeout above processing time, or call ChangeMessageVisibility as a heartbeat, and keep workers idempotent.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Billing, fraud detection and analytics must each process every event on the \u003ccode>payments\u003c/code> Kafka topic at their own pace. How should they consume it?","options":["Put all three services in one consumer group so they share the partitions","Give each service its own consumer group with independent offsets","Have billing consume and republish each event to the other two services","Create one partition per service so each reads only its own partition"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each consumer group gets every record and tracks its own offsets, so the services are independent. One shared group would split the records so each service sees only a fraction.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Which configuration ensures a record acknowledged to the producer survives the failure of any single broker, without trading away consistency during leader election?","options":["\u003ccode>acks=1\u003c/code>, replication factor 3, \u003ccode>min.insync.replicas=2\u003c/code>, unclean leader election enabled","\u003ccode>acks=all\u003c/code>, replication factor 2, \u003ccode>min.insync.replicas=1\u003c/code>, unclean leader election disabled","\u003ccode>acks=all\u003c/code>, replication factor 3, \u003ccode>min.insync.replicas=2\u003c/code>, unclean leader election disabled","\u003ccode>acks=0\u003c/code>, replication factor 3, \u003ccode>min.insync.replicas=2\u003c/code>, and fsync on every message"],"answer":2,"explain":"Every acknowledged record is on at least two replicas, and only an in-sync replica can become leader, so no acknowledged record is lost. With RF 2 and min ISR 1, the ISR can shrink to the leader alone and acks=all then means one copy. min.insync.replicas is enforced only for acks=all, so with acks=1 or acks=0 a record can be lost if the leader fails before followers copy it.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A consumer reads from a Kafka topic, inserts each payment into PostgreSQL, then commits its offset. The team enabled Kafka transactions on the producer that writes the topic. Can a payment be inserted twice?","options":["Yes: a crash between the insert and the offset commit redelivers the record","No: Kafka transactions give exactly-once delivery to every downstream system","No: read_committed isolation prevents a consumer from seeing a record twice","Yes, but only if unclean leader election is enabled and a leader fails"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kafka's exactly-once covers writes to Kafka and offset commits made inside the transaction, not a PostgreSQL insert. read_committed hides aborted records but does not stop redelivery after a consumer crash. Use a unique dedupe key on the insert, or store the offset in the same database transaction.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A 16-partition topic keyed by \u003ccode>user_id\u003c/code> is expanded to 32 partitions while running. What is the effect on ordering?","options":["Kafka rehashes and migrates existing records, so per-user ordering is preserved","Nothing changes, because keys stay bound to the partition they were first written to","Ordering is lost for every user until the old partitions are fully compacted","Many keys now map to a different partition, so per-user ordering breaks around the change"],"answer":3,"explain":"The partitioner computes hash mod partition count, so changing 16 to 32 remaps about half the keys, while existing data never moves. A consumer may process a user's new records before older ones still in the old partition. Plan partition counts ahead, or migrate to a new topic.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Producers write 50 MB/s on average into a Kafka cluster, with 7-day retention and replication factor 3. Roughly how much disk does the cluster need, before headroom?","options":["About 4 TB","About 30 TB","About 90 TB","About 300 TB"],"answer":2,"explain":"50 MB/s × 86,400 s ≈ 4.3 TB per day; × 7 days ≈ 30 TB; × 3 replicas ≈ 90 TB. A real plan adds 30–50% headroom for traffic growth and uneven partitions.","level":"hard"}],"storage-search-data":[{"q":"A social app stores user-uploaded videos, up to 2 GB each, which are written once and read many times through a CDN. Which storage fits best?","options":["Block storage volumes attached to the app servers","A shared NFS file system mounted on every app server","Object storage such as S3, used as the CDN origin","Blob columns in the PostgreSQL primary database"],"answer":2,"explain":"Write-once, read-many blobs are exactly what object storage is built for: unlimited scale, low cost per GB and native CDN origin support. Block volumes attach to one host and NFS adds cost and a shared bottleneck without any benefit for immutable files.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Your object store uses Reed-Solomon RS(6,3) erasure coding. What are the raw storage overhead and the number of fragment losses an object survives?","options":["1.5x raw storage, survives any 3 lost fragments","3x raw storage, survives any 2 lost fragments","1.5x raw storage, survives exactly 1 lost fragment","2x raw storage, survives any 6 lost fragments"],"answer":0,"explain":"RS(6,3) stores 6 data plus 3 parity fragments, each 1/6 of the object, so 9/6 = 1.5x, and any 6 of the 9 rebuild it, so any 3 can be lost. The 3x / 2 losses figures describe triple replication.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Why do media apps hand clients a presigned URL instead of proxying uploads through the API servers?","options":["Presigned URLs encrypt the file end to end, so the cloud provider can never read its contents","The bytes go straight to object storage, so app servers and their bandwidth stay out of the data path","Presigned URLs make the upload strongly consistent where a proxied upload would be eventually consistent","Object stores accept multipart uploads only from browsers, so servers cannot write large objects"],"answer":1,"explain":"A presigned URL is a time-limited, key-scoped signature that lets the client talk to the object store directly, keeping large transfers off your fleet. It is about authorization and offloading, not encryption or consistency.","level":"easy"},{"q":"What does an inverted index map?","options":["Each document id to the list of terms it contains, in order","Each document id to the disk page and byte offset where it is stored","Each search query to a cached, pre-ranked list of matching documents","Each term to the list of documents, and positions, where it appears"],"answer":3,"explain":"An inverted index goes from term to postings list, which is what makes \"which documents contain X\" fast. The document-to-terms direction is the forward index, which is what you would have to scan without it.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A user creates a product and, 200 ms later, searches for it in Elasticsearch but gets no hit. A second later it appears. What explains this?","options":["The document is not searchable until the next refresh, which runs every 1 s by default","The write went to a replica shard that had not yet forwarded it to the primary","The translog was not yet fsynced, so the write was lost and retried 1 s later","BM25 scores brand-new documents as zero until IDF statistics are recomputed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Elasticsearch is near-real-time: writes are durable in the translog immediately but only searchable after a refresh opens a new segment. Writes always go to the primary first, so the replica explanation is backwards.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your team wants to raise an existing Elasticsearch index from 5 to 8 primary shards in place. Why is this not a simple setting change?","options":["Lucene segments are encrypted with a per-shard key, and existing segments cannot be re-keyed","The new primaries would be promoted from existing replicas, which risks a split-brain election","Documents are placed by hash(routing) mod the primary shard count, so a new count remaps them","Elasticsearch embeds the shard count in every document's _id, so all ids would have to change"],"answer":2,"explain":"Routing is hash(routing) % number_of_primary_shards, so a new count would make lookups go to the wrong shard. You reindex into a new index (or use split/shrink, which only works with multiples or factors) and swap an alias.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Orders live in PostgreSQL and must be searchable in Elasticsearch. Developers occasionally see search results that are older than the database row. What is the most robust fix?","options":["Dual-write from the app in one try/catch, writing to Elasticsearch first and PostgreSQL second","Use CDC into Kafka keyed by order id, and index with the row version as the external version","Lower refresh_interval from 1 s to 10 ms so new documents become searchable sooner","Add more replicas and use adaptive replica selection so searches avoid stale copies"],"answer":1,"explain":"CDC makes the database log the single source of truth, per-key Kafka partitions keep updates in order, and external versioning rejects any stale write that arrives late. Reordering dual writes still leaves partial failures and races.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Compared with classic TF-IDF, what does BM25 change?","options":["It ignores document frequency and only counts term occurrences","It ranks by recency and click-through rate instead of term statistics","It requires training a learning-to-rank model on each index's click logs","It saturates term frequency and normalizes scores by document length"],"answer":3,"explain":"BM25 keeps IDF but caps the benefit of repeated terms (k1) and penalizes long documents relative to the average (b). Recency or click signals are business layers added on top, not part of BM25.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A clicks-per-minute dashboard shows a dip followed by a big spike right after the stream job recovered from a 5-minute outage. What is the likely cause and fix?","options":["Kafka dropped messages during the outage; fix it by enabling acks=all","The job had too few partitions; add partitions so the backlog drains faster","Windows were assigned by processing time; assign them by event time with a watermark","The sink double-wrote results on recovery; switch it to at-most-once delivery"],"answer":2,"explain":"With processing-time windows, the backlog consumed after recovery lands in the recovery minute, creating a false dip and spike. Event-time windows put each click in the minute it actually happened.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A sliding window has size 10 minutes and slide 1 minute. How many window instances does each event contribute to?","options":["10","1","11","It depends on the gap between events"],"answer":0,"explain":"A sliding window of size S with slide L puts each event in S / L windows, here 10. One window per event describes tumbling windows; gap-based membership describes session windows.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A Flink job reading a 32-partition Kafka topic has stopped emitting any windows, although events keep arriving on most partitions. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The checkpoint interval exceeds the window size, so windows are discarded before they fire","Idle partitions stop advancing their watermarks, which holds back the minimum watermark","The window operator's parallelism is below the partition count, so its windows never fire","RocksDB state outgrew memory, so the job silently fell back to processing-time windows"],"answer":1,"explain":"A source's watermark is the minimum over its partitions, so a partition with no traffic pins it in the past and no event-time window can close. Enabling idleness detection excludes idle partitions.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Analysts run \u003ccode>SUM(amount) GROUP BY region\u003c/code> over 5 billion rows of a 200-column table. Why is a columnar warehouse far faster than the row-store OLTP database?","options":["It keeps every table fully in RAM, so the 5 billion rows never touch the disk","It precomputes every GROUP BY at load time, so the query is just a lookup","It samples about 1% of the rows and returns an approximate SUM with an error bound","It reads only the columns used, which compress well and scan with vectorized code"],"answer":3,"explain":"Reading 2 of 200 columns cuts I/O by about 100x before compression, and similar values in a column compress 5 to 10x. Row stores read entire rows even when the query needs two fields.","level":"medium"},{"q":"In a Kappa architecture, how do you deploy a bug fix that must also correct historical results?","options":["Run a separate nightly Spark batch job over the archive that recomputes and overwrites history","Replay the retained log through the fixed job into a new output, then switch readers to it","Deploy the fix in place and patch the old output rows with one-off SQL UPDATE scripts","Raise the watermark delay to cover the history so old events flow through the fixed job"],"answer":1,"explain":"Kappa reprocesses by replaying the retained log through the same streaming code, then cutting over. A separate batch job for history is exactly the Lambda approach Kappa avoids.","level":"medium"},{"q":"What is the defining difference between ETL and ELT?","options":["ELT loads raw data into the warehouse first and transforms it there with SQL","ETL is used for streaming sources, while ELT is used only for nightly batch loads","ELT works only with structured tables, so JSON must be flattened before it is loaded","ETL keeps raw data in the warehouse and transforms it later with SQL"],"answer":0,"explain":"In ELT the warehouse does the transformation, so the raw data is kept and transformations can be rerun. ETL transforms before loading, which is still useful when data must be masked before it lands.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A MapReduce job that joins page views with users finishes 199 of 200 reduce tasks in 5 minutes, but the last one runs for 2 hours. What is the most likely cause and fix?","options":["One node has a failing disk; enable speculative execution so the task reruns elsewhere","The combiner is too aggressive and merges distinct keys; disable the combiner","Key skew from one hot key; salt that key or broadcast the smaller side of the join","Too few mappers; increase the input split count so map output arrives sooner"],"answer":2,"explain":"One straggling reducer usually means one key (a bot user, a null id) carries a disproportionate share of values. Speculative execution reruns the same overloaded task elsewhere and does not help.","level":"hard"}],"microservices-api":[{"q":"A 12-person startup is building its first product and the CTO wants 15 microservices from day one. What is the strongest argument against it?","options":["Kubernetes cannot route traffic between 15 services unless a service mesh such as Istio is installed","The team pays the cost of a distributed system before it knows where the real boundaries are","Every call between services adds about 50 ms, so pages built from several services feel slow","Monoliths scale only vertically, so the team should split only once the largest server is maxed out"],"answer":1,"explain":"Early boundaries are usually wrong, and wrong boundaries across a network are expensive to move. A modular monolith keeps refactoring cheap. A call inside one datacenter costs about a millisecond, not 50 ms, and monoliths can also scale horizontally behind a load balancer, so the last option is false.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Two services share one PostgreSQL schema. The Orders team renames a column and the Shipping service breaks in production. Which principle would have prevented this?","options":["Database per service, accessed only through the owner's API or events","An ORM in every service, so renamed columns are remapped automatically","Running each migration in a maintenance window with both services stopped","A read replica for Shipping, so it reads a stable copy of the schema"],"answer":0,"explain":"When each service owns its data, schema changes are private and the contract is the API. ORMs and maintenance windows do not remove the hidden coupling of a shared schema, and a streaming read replica receives the same rename.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Which pattern is characteristic of a \"distributed monolith\"?","options":["Services that publish domain events through a transactional outbox in the same database transaction","Services that are each owned end to end by one team, including on-call duty","Services that must deploy together and call each other synchronously in long chains","Services that use different languages, frameworks and databases from each other"],"answer":2,"explain":"Lockstep deploys and synchronous chains give you network failures and summed latency without independent deployability. Different languages per service are a normal microservices trait.","level":"easy"},{"q":"In Kubernetes, a pod calls \u003ccode>payments.prod.svc.cluster.local\u003c/code> and kube-proxy forwards to a healthy pod. What kind of discovery is this?","options":["Client-side discovery: the client resolves DNS and picks the pod it connects to","Peer-to-peer discovery: pods gossip their addresses to each other with no registry","Static configuration: the DNS name is fixed in config, so no discovery takes place","Server-side discovery: the platform routes a stable virtual IP to live pods"],"answer":3,"explain":"The client knows only a stable service name and virtual IP; the platform chooses the backend pod, which is server-side discovery. Client-side discovery means the client fetches the instance list and picks one itself.","level":"medium"},{"q":"The mobile app's home screen needs data from 6 services in one compact response, while the web app needs richer payloads from the same services. What is the cleanest design?","options":["Client-specific branches in every backend service, selected by the User-Agent header","A backend for frontend per client that fans out in parallel and shapes responses","Direct calls from the mobile app to all 6 services, sent in parallel over HTTP/2","One merged service built from the 6, so a single call returns every field"],"answer":1,"explain":"A BFF owned by each client team aggregates and tailors responses without polluting services or the shared gateway. Direct calls from a phone expose internal services and multiply requests over a high-latency network, even when sent in parallel.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A security team requires that all service-to-service traffic be encrypted and mutually authenticated across 300 services written in 5 languages. What is the most practical approach?","options":["A service mesh such as Istio or Linkerd, with sidecars handling mTLS and certificate rotation","TLS terminated at the edge API gateway, with plain HTTP inside the cluster","A TLS library added by each team, with each service managing its own certificates","One private subnet for all services, with security groups in place of encryption"],"answer":0,"explain":"A mesh enforces mTLS uniformly without app changes and automates certificate rotation. Per-team libraries mean five implementations and 300 services managing certificates by hand; edge TLS only protects north-south traffic, and network isolation alone does not authenticate services.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A client sends \u003ccode>POST /payments\u003c/code> with an \u003ccode>Idempotency-Key\u003c/code>. The first attempt is still processing when an identical retry arrives with the same key. What should the server do?","options":["Process the retry as a new payment, since the first has not finished","Return 200 with an empty body so the client stops retrying","Return 409 Conflict, or wait, without running the payment again","Delete the stored key and restart the payment from the beginning"],"answer":2,"explain":"The key is claimed atomically as in progress, so a concurrent duplicate must not execute again; 409 tells the client to retry later and then get the stored result. Processing it again is exactly the double charge the key exists to prevent.","level":"medium"},{"q":"An endpoint uses \u003ccode>LIMIT 50 OFFSET n\u003c/code> on a 200M-row table. Page 1 takes 5 ms but page 20,000 takes 4 seconds. Why, and what fixes it?","options":["The B-tree becomes unbalanced at high offsets; rebuild the index with REINDEX","PostgreSQL caches only the first pages of a table; raise shared_buffers to 32 GB","Offsets above 1M rows fall outside the index; range-partition the table by created_at month","Each skipped row is read and discarded; use keyset pagination on an indexed (created_at, id)"],"answer":3,"explain":"OFFSET still walks past every skipped row, so cost grows with depth. A keyset predicate lets the index seek directly to the last seen position, keeping each page constant time.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Which API change is backward compatible for existing clients that ignore unknown fields?","options":["Adding a new optional field to a response","Renaming \u003ccode>user_name\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>username\u003c/code>","Changing \u003ccode>price\u003c/code> from a string to an integer","Making a previously optional request field required"],"answer":0,"explain":"Additive, optional changes do not break tolerant clients. Renames, type changes and stricter validation all break existing integrations and require a new version.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A limit of 100 requests per minute uses a fixed window counter. What is the most requests a client can get accepted within about one second?","options":["100","101","150","200"],"answer":3,"explain":"The client can send 100 at the end of one window and 100 more at the start of the next, all within about a second. Sliding window log or counter, or a token bucket with capacity below 200, prevents this edge burst.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A token bucket has capacity 20 and refills at 5 tokens per second. A client that has been idle for a minute sends requests as fast as it can. Roughly how many are accepted in the first 2 seconds?","options":["10","20","30","40"],"answer":2,"explain":"The bucket starts full at 20 (refill is capped at capacity, so idle time does not add more), and 2 seconds of refill at 5 per second adds about 10, so about 30. Answering 10 ignores the burst capacity.","level":"hard"},{"q":"With a sliding window counter limit of 100 per minute, the previous minute had 60 requests, the current minute has 50, and we are 30 seconds into the current minute. Is the next request allowed?","options":["Yes, the estimate is 80","Yes, the current window has only 50 requests","No, the estimate is 110","No, the estimate is exactly 100"],"answer":0,"explain":"Half of the previous window still overlaps, so 60 x 0.5 + 50 = 80, under 100. Looking only at the current window ignores the previous minute's traffic, which is the fixed-window flaw this algorithm fixes.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Ten gateway instances each run \u003ccode>GET count\u003c/code>, compare it to the limit in application code, then \u003ccode>INCR\u003c/code> in Redis. Under load, clients exceed their limit. Why?","options":["INCR is not atomic in Redis Cluster, so two nodes can increment the same key at once","GET and INCR are separate steps, so concurrent requests all read the same old count","Redis evicts the counters under memory pressure, which resets them to zero","Redis is single-threaded, so it drops INCR commands that queue up under load"],"answer":1,"explain":"Check-then-act across two round trips is a race: many requests see 99 and all proceed. Do the check and update atomically, with INCR first and compare its result, or with a Lua script.","level":"hard"},{"q":"The Redis cluster backing your public API's rate limiter becomes unreachable. For most product APIs, what is the usual default and its main risk?","options":["Fail closed, rejecting every request with 429 until Redis returns; risk is a full API outage","Switch to fixed-window counters in PostgreSQL; risk is extra load on the primary database","Fail open with a coarse per-gateway in-memory limit; risk is backends absorbing excess traffic","Queue requests in the gateway until Redis returns; risk is memory exhaustion and timeouts"],"answer":2,"explain":"Turning a limiter outage into a full API outage is usually worse than briefly weaker limiting, so most APIs fail open with a coarse local limit as a safety net. Abuse-sensitive limits such as login attempts or SMS sends often fail closed instead.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Thousands of users behind one mobile carrier's NAT get 429s although each makes only a few requests. What is the best fix?","options":["Exempt known mobile carrier IP ranges from rate limiting entirely","Raise the per-IP limit tenfold for everyone, so shared IPs have headroom","Switch the per-IP limiter from fixed windows to a leaky bucket so bursts are smoothed out","Limit per authenticated user or API key, with only a loose per-IP limit at the edge"],"answer":3,"explain":"Carrier-grade NAT puts many users behind one IP, so IP is a poor identity for authenticated traffic. Changing the algorithm does not fix a wrong key.","level":"medium"}],"reliability":[{"q":"A request must pass through a load balancer (99.99%), an app server (99.9%) and a database (99.9%). None is redundant. What is the approximate end-to-end availability?","options":["99.99%, set by the most reliable component","99.9%, set by the least reliable component","99.79%, the product of all three","99.999%, because there are three layers of protection"],"answer":2,"explain":"Components in series multiply: 0.9999 × 0.999 × 0.999 ≈ 0.9979, about 18 hours of downtime a year. A series system is always less available than its weakest part, not equal to it.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Two independent replicas, each 99.9% available, sit behind a load balancer and either can serve any request. Ignoring the load balancer and failover time, what is the availability of the replica tier?","options":["99.8%","99.9%","99.95%","99.9999%"],"answer":3,"explain":"Parallel components fail only if all fail: 1 − (0.001 × 0.001) = 0.999999. The 99.8% answer is the series formula, which applies when both are required.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Your service needs 12 servers to handle peak traffic. You spread it evenly across 3 availability zones and must survive losing any one AZ at peak with no degradation. What is the minimum total server count?","options":["12","18","16","24"],"answer":1,"explain":"After losing one AZ, the remaining two must hold 12 servers, so each AZ needs 6, giving 18 total (N × k / (k − 1) = 12 × 3 / 2). With only 2 AZs you would need 24.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A client calls \u003ccode>POST /payments\u003c/code>, times out after 5 seconds and retries. The customer is charged twice. What is the right fix?","options":["Send an idempotency key with each payment and have the server dedupe on it","Raise the client timeout to 60 seconds so the first attempt can always finish","Retry with exponential backoff and jitter instead of retrying immediately","Change the endpoint from \u003ccode>POST\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>GET\u003c/code> so it becomes safe to retry"],"answer":0,"explain":"A timeout does not tell the client whether the server did the work, so retries of a non-idempotent call can duplicate it. An idempotency key makes the retry return the original result. Backoff only changes when the duplicate happens, and a longer timeout still fails sometimes.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Why add jitter to exponential backoff?","options":["To guarantee that the total retry time stays within the caller's deadline","To make each individual retry more likely to succeed than the one before","To spread retries from many clients so they do not arrive in waves","To stop clients from retrying non-idempotent requests such as POST"],"answer":2,"explain":"Without jitter, clients that failed together retry together, hitting the recovering server in bursts. Randomizing each wait spreads the load out; capping the backoff, not jitter, is what bounds the total wait.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A 30-second database blip became a 40-minute outage. During the incident the database saw about 20x normal traffic. The API gateway, order service and inventory service each make up to 3 attempts per call. What change helps most?","options":["Raise each layer to 5 attempts with longer backoff so more requests eventually succeed","Add two more read replicas so the database has spare capacity during incidents","Lower every timeout to 10 ms so failed calls release resources faster","Retry only at the layer nearest the database, with a cap and a retry budget"],"answer":3,"explain":"Retries multiply across layers: 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 database calls per user request, a retry storm that keeps the database from recovering. Retrying at one layer under a budget (for example at most 10% extra) bounds the amplification; more attempts make it worse.","level":"hard"},{"q":"What does a circuit breaker do in the \u003cb>half-open\u003c/b> state?","options":["Lets all calls through while it keeps counting failures in a sliding window","Lets a few trial calls through; success closes it, failure reopens it","Fails every call fast until an operator resets it from the dashboard","Sends calls to a standby region while the primary dependency recovers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Half-open is the probe phase after the open cool-down expires. Allowing only a few calls tests recovery without flooding a dependency that may still be fragile; letting everything through is the closed state.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A product page service uses one shared pool of 200 threads for all downstream calls. The recommendation service starts hanging until its 30-second timeout, and soon checkout requests on the same service fail too. Which pattern most directly prevents checkout from failing?","options":["A bulkhead: a separate, bounded pool for each dependency","Doubling the shared pool to 400 threads so it has spare room","Adding three retries with backoff to every recommendation call","Caching checkout responses in Redis so checkout skips the pool"],"answer":0,"explain":"At 20 recommendation calls per second and 30 seconds each, Little's Law gives 600 calls in flight, which exhausts 200 or 400 shared threads. A bulkhead caps recommendations to its own small pool so checkout keeps its threads; retries would add load.","level":"medium"},{"q":"During a traffic spike your servers are at 100% CPU and completing requests at full speed, yet nearly every client sees a timeout. What is happening and what fixes it?","options":["The load balancer algorithm is wrong; switch from least connections to round robin so load evens out","Client timeouts are too short; raise them to 60 seconds so slow requests have time to finish","Servers work on requests whose clients gave up; bound queues, drop expired requests, shed load early","The database is missing indexes; add them so each request needs less CPU and finishes sooner"],"answer":2,"explain":"This is goodput collapse: throughput stays high but the work is wasted on abandoned requests. Rejecting excess load cheaply and skipping requests past their deadline keeps the work you do useful; longer timeouts just make queues deeper.","level":"hard"},{"q":"What does a recovery point objective (RPO) of 5 minutes mean?","options":["Service must be back online within 5 minutes of a disaster","At most the last 5 minutes of writes may be lost","Backups must be taken and restore-tested every 5 minutes","Failover must be detected and started within 5 minutes"],"answer":1,"explain":"RPO bounds data loss, measured backward from the disaster to the last safe copy. Restoring service within a time limit is the RTO.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A service needs an RTO under 10 minutes for a full regional outage and can tolerate about 1 minute of data loss. The team wants the cheapest strategy that meets both. Which one?","options":["Backup and restore: nightly snapshots copied to the second region","Pilot light: replicated data, no running app tier","Warm standby: a scaled-down working stack that scales up","Multi-site active-active: full capacity serving in both regions"],"answer":2,"explain":"Warm standby keeps a running stack with continuously replicated data, so failover is mostly scaling up and redirecting traffic, achievable in minutes. Pilot light must provision compute first, typically tens of minutes; active-active meets the target but costs more.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A buggy migration corrupts 10% of rows in your PostgreSQL database. You have a synchronous standby in another AZ, an asynchronous replica in another region, nightly base backups and continuous WAL archiving. How do you recover the data?","options":["Fail over to the synchronous standby, which holds the last committed state","Promote the cross-region asynchronous replica, which lags a few seconds","Restart the primary so it replays the WAL from its last checkpoint","Restore a base backup and replay archived WAL to just before the migration"],"answer":3,"explain":"Replicas apply every change, including the corrupting writes, within milliseconds or seconds. Point-in-time recovery from a backup plus the WAL archive is what restores the state before a bad write; replication protects only against losing hardware.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A stateless tier runs active-active on two nodes, each at 70% CPU at peak. One node fails at peak. What happens?","options":["The survivor needs about 140% CPU and overloads; keep each node under 50% or add a third","The survivor stays near 70% because the load balancer queues the excess requests","Nothing visible: in active-active each node already has capacity for all traffic","Autoscaling launches a replacement within seconds, so almost no requests are affected"],"answer":0,"explain":"Active-active only survives a failure if the remaining nodes can absorb the lost share. With two nodes each must run below about 50% at peak; with three, below about 66%. New instances take minutes to boot, so autoscaling does not cover the gap.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Before promoting a database standby during failover, why must you fence the old primary?","options":["To shorten the time the standby takes to catch up on replication","To prevent split brain, with both nodes accepting writes","To flush the DNS cache so clients find the new primary faster","To free disk space on the old primary before it is rebuilt"],"answer":1,"explain":"A primary that is only slow or partitioned may still be accepting writes. Fencing (revoking its lease, cutting its network or storage access, or using fencing tokens) guarantees a single writer.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A component fails on average every 1,000 hours and takes 1 hour to repair. Which change improves its availability more?","options":["Doubling MTBF to 2,000 hours with better hardware","Adding a second hour of scheduled maintenance each month","Doubling both MTBF and MTTR together","Cutting MTTR to 6 minutes with automated failover"],"answer":3,"explain":"Availability is MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR). Doubling MTBF gives 2000 / 2001 ≈ 99.95%; cutting MTTR to 0.1 h gives 1000 / 1000.1 ≈ 99.99%. Doubling both leaves the ratio unchanged.","level":"hard"}],"observability-security":[{"q":"Which metric type should you use for the current number of messages waiting in a queue?","options":["Counter","Gauge","Histogram","Summary"],"answer":1,"explain":"Queue depth goes up and down and you care about its current value, which is exactly what a gauge records. A counter only increases, so it suits totals such as messages enqueued, whose rate you graph.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Your service has a 99.9% availability SLO measured monthly. Roughly how much total downtime does the error budget allow per month?","options":["About 4.4 minutes","About 8.8 hours","About 7.2 hours","About 44 minutes"],"answer":3,"explain":"The budget is 0.1% of the month: 0.001 × 43,830 minutes ≈ 43.8 minutes (43.2 for a 30-day window). 4.4 minutes is the budget for 99.99%, and 8.8 hours is a year at 99.9%.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A logged-in user requests another tenant's invoice. Their token is valid but they have no access to that invoice. Which status code is correct?","options":["\u003ccode>403 Forbidden\u003c/code>: the identity is known but lacks permission","\u003ccode>401 Unauthorized\u003c/code>: the user lacks rights to this invoice","\u003ccode>400 Bad Request\u003c/code>: the invoice ID is outside the caller's tenant","\u003ccode>429 Too Many Requests\u003c/code>: the user is probing for other IDs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Despite its name, 401 means the caller has not been authenticated (missing or invalid credentials). Here authentication succeeded and authorization failed, which is 403. Some APIs return 404 instead so they do not reveal that the resource exists.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Which is the best way to store user passwords in a new system?","options":["Encrypt them with AES-256 using a key stored in KMS","Hash them with SHA-256 and a global secret pepper","Hash them with Argon2id using a unique random salt per user","Store them in a separate database with strict access controls"],"answer":2,"explain":"Passwords only need to be verified, so store a one-way hash, and use a slow, memory-hard, salted algorithm so each guess is expensive. SHA-256 is fast enough for GPUs to try billions of guesses per second, and encryption is reversible by anyone with the key.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Ten app servers each report a p99 latency gauge. Your dashboard shows the average of the ten values as the fleet p99. What is wrong and how do you fix it?","options":["Nothing; the mean of per-server p99s equals the fleet p99","The mean hides outliers; the maximum of the ten p99s is the true fleet p99","The p99 is too noisy; average the ten p50 values instead","Percentiles don't average; merge histogram buckets, then take p99"],"answer":3,"explain":"A percentile of the whole fleet depends on the full distribution, which averages of per-server percentiles lose, especially when servers carry different traffic. Histogram buckets add up across servers, so the fleet percentile can be computed from them; the maximum is only an upper bound.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your SLO is 99.95% of requests succeeding over 30 days, and the service handles 40M requests in that window. An incident caused 8,000 failed requests. How much error budget remains?","options":["About 20% of the budget","About 60% of the budget","About 80% of the budget","None; the SLO is already breached"],"answer":1,"explain":"The budget is 0.0005 × 40M = 20,000 failed requests. The incident used 8,000, which is 40%, leaving 12,000, or 60%.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Which of these conditions should page the on-call engineer at 3 a.m.?","options":["CPU on one read replica of the orders database has stayed above 90% for 10 minutes","One of six stateless app instances failed its health check and was replaced","The checkout error budget is burning at 14.4 times the sustainable rate","Garbage collection pauses on the search cluster rose from 20 ms to 60 ms"],"answer":2,"explain":"Pages should fire on symptoms that threaten the SLO and need human action now. The others are causes that may never affect users; put them on dashboards or tickets. A fast burn rate means the budget will be gone within days.","level":"medium"},{"q":"You want to keep every trace that contains an error or is slower than 2 seconds, while storing only about 1% of normal traces. Which sampling approach achieves this?","options":["Tail-based sampling in a collector, decided after each trace completes","Head-based sampling at 1%, decided at the edge and propagated in the trace context","Head-based sampling at 100% at the edge, deleting traces after one day","Each service independently keeps 1% of its spans plus every span with an error"],"answer":0,"explain":"Head-based sampling decides before anyone knows whether the request will fail or be slow, so it keeps only 1% of errors too. Independent per-service sampling produces broken, partial traces, even when each service keeps its own error spans. Tail sampling buffers the spans and decides with the full outcome known.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your API issues JWT access tokens valid for 24 hours. An employee is fired, but their token keeps working for the rest of the day. What is the most practical fix?","options":["Switch from RS256 to HS256 so tokens are verified with a shared secret","Encrypt the payload as a JWE so a leftover token cannot be replayed","Use 10-minute access tokens, revocable refresh tokens and a small denylist","Rotate the signing key every time any user account is disabled"],"answer":2,"explain":"A JWT is valid until it expires unless verifiers check something extra. Short access tokens bound the exposure, revoking the server-side refresh token stops renewal, and a small denylist checked at the gateway cuts access in seconds. Rotating the key logs out every user, and the algorithm or encryption does not affect revocation.","level":"medium"},{"q":"You are designing permissions for a Google Drive-like product: users share files and folders with people and groups, and folder access is inherited by everything inside. Which authorization model fits best?","options":["RBAC with a role per folder, such as \u003ccode>folder-123-editor\u003c/code>, granted to groups","ABAC policies comparing each user's department attribute with the file's department","ACLs on each file, recopied down the tree whenever a parent folder's sharing changes","ReBAC: store relationship tuples and derive access through groups and folders"],"answer":3,"explain":"Access here is defined by relationships (member of group, editor of folder, folder is parent of file), which is what Zanzibar-style ReBAC models directly. Per-folder roles explode in number, and copying ACLs on every change is slow and error-prone for large trees.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A single-page app calls your API with the access token in the \u003ccode>Authorization: Bearer\u003c/code> header, held in memory. A security review asks whether the API needs CSRF protection. What is the right answer?","options":["Yes, because every state-changing endpoint needs a CSRF token","No, because a forged cross-site request cannot carry the in-memory bearer token","Yes, because the browser adds the bearer token to every request to that domain","No, because TLS encryption on its own stops cross-site request forgery"],"answer":1,"explain":"CSRF works because browsers attach cookies automatically to requests triggered by other sites. A header the app sets explicitly is not sent by a forged form or image request. If the API also accepted cookie authentication, it would need SameSite cookies and CSRF tokens; HTTPS does nothing against CSRF.","level":"medium"},{"q":"With a 30-day SLO window, an incident burns error budget at a steady burn rate of 14.4. If nothing changes, roughly how long until the entire monthly budget is gone?","options":["About 50 hours","About 14 hours","About 5 days","About 2 hours"],"answer":0,"explain":"A burn rate of 1 spends the budget in exactly 30 days, or 720 hours. At 14.4 it lasts 720 / 14.4 = 50 hours, which means 2% of the budget per hour, the standard fast-burn paging threshold.","level":"hard"},{"q":"In the OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow, what specific attack does PKCE prevent?","options":["An attacker stealing the access token from the API's memory","A user being tricked into approving excessive scopes on the consent screen","An attacker replaying an expired ID token to log in later","An attacker redeeming an intercepted authorization code for tokens"],"answer":3,"explain":"The client sends a hash of a random code_verifier at the start and must present the verifier itself when exchanging the code. An attacker who intercepts the redirect gets only the code and the hash, and cannot produce the verifier. Replay of ID tokens is handled by the nonce and expiry, not PKCE.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A service stores 50 TB of customer documents and wants KMS-managed encryption with yearly key rotation. A teammate proposes sending each document to the KMS \u003ccode>Encrypt\u003c/code> API. What is the better design?","options":["Use the KMS master key directly in the app by exporting it once at startup","Encrypt each document with a key derived from the customer's password via PBKDF2","Use envelope encryption: encrypt locally with a data key that KMS wraps","Skip application encryption, because the cloud provider already encrypts the disks"],"answer":2,"explain":"KMS APIs are rate limited, add a network round trip and accept only small payloads (4 KB for AWS KMS Encrypt), so bulk data is encrypted locally with a data key that KMS wraps, and only the wrapped (encrypted) data key is stored alongside each document. Rotating the master key then means re-wrapping small keys, not re-encrypting 50 TB. Master keys should never leave the KMS.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Your link-preview feature fetches any URL a user pastes. An attacker submits \u003ccode>http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/\u003c/code>. What is the attack, and what is the strongest defense?","options":["SSRF; block private and link-local IPs on every lookup and redirect, and require IMDSv2","XSS; HTML-encode the fetched title and description before rendering the preview","SQL injection; bind the pasted URL as a query parameter when saving the preview","CSRF; require a SameSite cookie and a CSRF token on the preview endpoint"],"answer":0,"explain":"The server is being tricked into requesting the cloud metadata service from inside the network, which can return IAM credentials. Blocking internal destinations (rechecking after redirects and DNS resolution), isolating the fetcher and using IMDSv2 close the hole; the other defenses address different attacks.","level":"hard"}],"advanced-techniques":[{"q":"In the classic Snowflake layout (1 sign bit, 41 timestamp bits, 10 machine bits, 12 sequence bits), how many IDs can one worker issue in a single millisecond before it must wait for the next one?","options":["1,024","4,096","65,536","2^22, about 4.2 million"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 12-bit sequence runs from 0 to 4,095, so one worker can issue 4,096 IDs per millisecond, about 4.1 million per second. 1,024 is the number of workers the 10 machine bits allow, not the per-millisecond capacity.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Roughly how much memory does one Redis HyperLogLog use, and what is its standard error?","options":["About 1 KB, with about 5% standard error","It grows with the number of distinct members and has no error","About 1.2 MB, with about 0.01% standard error","About 12 KB, with about 0.81% standard error"],"answer":3,"explain":"Redis uses 16,384 six-bit registers (12 KB), and the error is 1.04 / √16384 ≈ 0.81% no matter how many distinct items are added. An exact set would grow with cardinality, which is exactly what HLL avoids.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A cluster of N nodes spreads membership changes by gossip: every second each node exchanges state with a random peer. Roughly how long does a new fact take to reach every node?","options":["O(log N) rounds, about 10 to 20 for 1,000 nodes","O(N) rounds, about 1,000 for 1,000 nodes","One round, because every node talks to every other node","O(N²) rounds, because each pair must exchange state"],"answer":0,"explain":"As in an epidemic, the number of informed nodes roughly doubles each round, so dissemination takes a logarithmic number of rounds while each node sends a constant number of messages per round. All-to-all exchange would take one round but O(N²) messages, which is what gossip avoids.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A signup service checks a Bloom filter containing every username ever registered before it queries the database. Which outcome can \u003cb>never\u003c/b> happen?","options":["The filter says a name is taken, but the database has no such user","The false-positive rate rises as more names are registered","The filter says a name is free, but the database already has that name","Two different names share some of the same bit positions"],"answer":2,"explain":"A Bloom filter has no false negatives: adding a name set all of its bits, so a 0 bit proves the name was never added. False positives, the first option, do happen and become more likely as the filter fills, which is why a 'maybe' answer still goes to the database.","level":"easy"},{"q":"You need a Bloom filter for 1 billion crawled URLs with a 1% false-positive rate. About how much memory does it need?","options":["About 120 MB","About 1.2 GB","About 12 GB","About 100 GB, since URLs average 100 bytes"],"answer":1,"explain":"At 1% you need about 9.6 bits per item: 9.6 × 10^9 bits ≈ 1.2 GB, with k ≈ 7 hash functions. The size depends only on n and p, never on how long the items are, so the last option is a trap.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A Count-Min Sketch reports an estimate of 1,200 for \u003ccode>#launch\u003c/code>. What do you know about its true count?","options":["It is exactly 1,200","It is at least 1,200","It is at most 1,200","It is within about ±0.81% of 1,200, either way"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each counter holds the true count plus collisions from other keys, so the minimum across rows can only overestimate: the true count is at most 1,200, and with probability 1 − δ at least 1,200 − εN. The ±0.81% figure belongs to HyperLogLog, which estimates distinct counts, not frequencies.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your dashboard keeps one Redis HyperLogLog of visitor IDs per day. Product wants the number of unique visitors over the last 7 days. What is correct?","options":["\u003ccode>PFMERGE\u003c/code> the seven daily keys and run \u003ccode>PFCOUNT\u003c/code> on the result","Add up the seven daily \u003ccode>PFCOUNT\u003c/code> values for the weekly total","Average the seven daily counts and multiply by 7","It cannot be done; recount from the raw logs each time"],"answer":0,"explain":"Merging HLLs takes the register-wise maximum, which produces the sketch of the union, so a visitor seen on several days is counted once. Adding daily counts counts a returning visitor once per day and overstates weekly uniques.","level":"medium"},{"q":"An NTP correction steps a Snowflake worker's clock back by 5 ms. What should the generator do?","options":["Keep issuing IDs and reset the sequence to 0","Switch to a random 12-bit sequence until the clock recovers","Add a random offset to the timestamp to avoid collisions","Pause issuing IDs until the clock passes the last timestamp it used"],"answer":3,"explain":"Issuing IDs for a millisecond the worker has already used can repeat a (timestamp, machine, sequence) triple, which is a duplicate ID. Waiting out a small backward jump, or refusing and alerting on a large one, preserves uniqueness; running NTP in slew mode avoids the step in the first place.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A nearby-restaurants query looks up only the user's own precision-6 geohash cell and misses a restaurant 80 m away. What is the most likely cause and fix?","options":["Precision 6 is too coarse; query the finer precision-9 cell instead","It is across a cell boundary; also query the 8 neighbouring cells","Geohash ignores longitude near the user; use latitude-only ranges","Cached cell results are stale; lower the cache TTL on cell lookups"],"answer":1,"explain":"Points that are close together can sit in different cells, sometimes with no shared prefix at all, so proximity search queries the 3 × 3 block around the user and then filters by true distance. A finer precision makes the miss more likely, not less, because the cells get smaller.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Two Cassandra replicas of a 500 GB token range need repair. How does a Merkle tree keep the data transferred small?","options":["Compare hashes from the root down and stream only ranges whose hashes differ","Compress the full 500 GB range on both replicas and exchange the compressed copies","Send a Bloom filter of all keys so each replica can see which keys the other lacks","Exchange per-key timestamps and keep the newer version of every key on both sides"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal hashes prove a subtree matches, so the comparison prunes everything identical and only mismatched leaf ranges are streamed. Exchanging per-key timestamps or full copies moves data proportional to the whole range even when only a few rows differ, and a Bloom filter cannot detect keys whose values differ.","level":"medium"},{"q":"You are building a notes app where a user edits on a phone and a laptop that may both be offline for days, then syncs peer-to-peer or through any relay. Which approach fits best?","options":["Operational transformation with a central server ordering every edit","Last-write-wins on the whole document using device clocks","A CRDT-based document model such as Yjs or Automerge","Pessimistic locking so only one device can edit at a time"],"answer":2,"explain":"CRDTs converge whatever order or route updates arrive by, which suits long offline periods and peer-to-peer sync. OT in practice needs a central server to impose one order, and whole-document last-write-wins silently discards one device's edits.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A MySQL InnoDB table uses random UUID v4 primary keys, and insert throughput drops sharply once the table outgrows the buffer pool. Which change fixes the root cause with the least redesign?","options":["Add a secondary index on the UUID column to speed up lookups","Store the UUID as a 36-character string instead of 16 binary bytes","Shard the table by the first byte of the UUID","Switch to time-ordered keys such as UUID v7 or Snowflake IDs"],"answer":3,"explain":"InnoDB clusters rows by primary key, so random keys insert into random leaf pages that must be read from disk and split, while time-ordered keys append at the right edge where the pages stay in memory. Storing the key as a string makes every index entry bigger, and sharding spreads the problem around without removing it.","level":"hard"},{"q":"You move an events table to Bigtable with row keys that start with a Snowflake ID. Adding nodes does not raise write throughput, and one node is pegged. Why?","options":["Snowflake IDs are not unique across data centers","Increasing keys all land in the tablet owning the newest range","Bigtable cannot index 64-bit integer row keys efficiently","The 12-bit sequence caps the table at 4,096 writes per millisecond"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bigtable partitions rows by contiguous key ranges, so monotonically increasing keys always hit the last tablet, a moving hot spot. Lead the key with something well distributed, such as a hash prefix, a salt or a bit-reversed ID; this is also why Spanner advises against sequential primary keys.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Twenty web servers behind a round-robin load balancer each count hashtags for the last hour and send only their local top 10 to a merger, which adds them up and returns the top 10. What is wrong?","options":["A tag ranked 11th on every server can be first overall yet appear in no list","Adding counts double-counts tags that appear in several local lists","Round robin delivers each event to several servers, inflating counts","Nothing: the union of the local top-10 lists always contains the global top 10"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because a hashtag's events are spread across all servers, its total can be the largest while it ranks 11th on every server. Partition events by hashtag first, for example with a Kafka topic keyed by tag, so each aggregator holds complete counts, or send full counts instead of local top-K lists.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Why does Cassandra use a phi accrual failure detector rather than a fixed heartbeat timeout?","options":["It guarantees a crashed node is never confused with a slow one","It removes heartbeats and infers liveness from client traffic","It adapts suspicion to each peer's observed heartbeat timing","It needs no threshold, because the right value is derived automatically"],"answer":2,"explain":"Phi accrual models each peer's heartbeat inter-arrival times and outputs a suspicion level φ, where φ = 3 means about a 0.1% chance that suspecting the peer is a mistake. No detector can perfectly tell crashed from slow in an asynchronous network, and it still needs a threshold: Cassandra's \u003ccode>phi_convict_threshold\u003c/code> defaults to 8.","level":"hard"}],"case-studies-core":[{"q":"A URL shortener expects about 182 billion links over 10 years and uses base62 codes. What is the shortest code length that fits?","options":["6 characters","7 characters","8 characters","10 characters"],"answer":1,"explain":"62^6 ≈ 56.8 billion is too small, while 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion fits with about 19x headroom. Eight characters would also work but wastes a character on every link without any need.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Why does a URL shortener handling thousands of creates per second prefer a pre-generated key service over hashing the long URL and checking for collisions?","options":["Hash digests are binary and cannot be encoded in the base62 alphabet at all","A key service produces shorter codes than a truncated hash can","Uniqueness is settled offline, so creates need no read-before-write or retry","A key service lets identical long URLs share a single short code"],"answer":2,"explain":"Truncated hashes collide, and the collision rate grows as the key space fills, so every create needs a check or conditional write plus retries. A KGS hands out keys already known to be unused in batches, which keeps the write path to one local pop and one insert. Sharing a code for identical URLs is a property of hashing, not of the KGS.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Your shortener sells per-click analytics and lets owners disable links instantly. Which redirect status should it return?","options":["302 Found, because browsers do not cache it unless headers allow it","301 Moved Permanently, because a short link never changes its target","200 OK with a meta-refresh page, because it can run analytics scripts","308 Permanent Redirect, because it preserves the HTTP method and body"],"answer":0,"explain":"Browsers may cache a 301 indefinitely, so repeat visits never reach the service, which breaks analytics and makes disabling a link ineffective. 308 is also permanent and cacheable. A meta-refresh page adds a full page load to every redirect.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A single short link goes viral and sends 50k redirects/s, all of which hit one Redis key and saturate that shard. What is the most effective fix?","options":["Add shards to the Redis cluster so the key's load is spread out","Switch every link from 302 to 301 so browsers cache redirects","Move the links table to a larger database instance with more IOPS","Cache hot codes in an in-process LRU on each redirect server"],"answer":3,"explain":"A single key lives on one shard however many shards exist, so adding shards does not spread it. A local LRU with a short TTL on every redirect server serves the hot code from memory, at the cost of a disable taking up to the TTL to apply. Switching to 301 would give up the analytics the product sells.","level":"medium"},{"q":"In a Dynamo-style store with N = 3, which setting guarantees that a read quorum overlaps the most recent successful write quorum?","options":["R = 1, W = 1","R = 1, W = 2","R = 2, W = 1","R = 2, W = 2"],"answer":3,"explain":"Overlap requires R + W > N. Only R = 2, W = 2 gives 4 > 3; the other three settings sum to 3 or less, so a read can land entirely on replicas that missed the write.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Two replicas hold versions of the same key with vector clocks \u003ccode>[Sx:2, Sy:1]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[Sx:2, Sz:1]\u003c/code>. What should the store do on a read?","options":["Keep the version with the higher wall-clock timestamp","Return both versions as siblings for the client to merge","Keep \u003ccode>[Sx:2, Sy:1]\u003c/code>, since Sy sorts before Sz in node order","Discard both and return their common ancestor \u003ccode>[Sx:2]\u003c/code>"],"answer":1,"explain":"One version descends from another only if all its counters are greater than or equal to the other's. Here each has a counter the other lacks, so the writes were concurrent and both must be kept for the client to merge. Picking by timestamp is last-write-wins, which silently drops one update.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A cluster uses N = 3, R = 2, W = 2 with sloppy quorums. During a network partition, a write is acknowledged by node A and fallback nodes D and E. Right after the partition heals, before any background process has run, a read of the same key from home replicas B and C returns the old value. Why?","options":["Two of the three acks came from fallback nodes, so the quorums need not overlap","Vector clocks on B and C rejected the newer version as a concurrent sibling","Read repair always runs before the coordinator returns, so this cannot happen","Anti-entropy with Merkle trees reverted the write as it reached B and C"],"answer":0,"explain":"Quorum overlap is defined over the N home replicas. A sloppy quorum counts fallback nodes toward W to stay writable, which breaks that overlap until hinted handoff copies the write back to B and C. This is the availability-for-consistency trade Dynamo makes on purpose.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A Cassandra node was offline for 14 days. \u003ccode>gc_grace_seconds\u003c/code> is the default 10 days, and some keys were deleted while it was down. What is the risk if you simply restart it and run repair?","options":["Repair will fail because the node's token range was reassigned to its neighbors","The node will refuse reads until its whole commit log has been replayed","Deleted data can reappear, since the tombstones were already purged elsewhere","The deleted keys will be duplicated as siblings across every replica"],"answer":2,"explain":"Tombstones exist so a delete can reach every replica before it is purged. Once the grace period passes, the healthy replicas drop them, and the returning node's stale values look like live data that anti-entropy should spread. Repair more often than the grace period, and rebuild a node that was gone longer than it.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Why do distributed crawlers usually assign URLs to nodes by hashing the \u003cb>hostname\u003c/b> rather than the full URL?","options":["Hostname hashing balances load across nodes better than full-URL hashing","Each host's robots.txt and politeness timer are owned by a single node","It lets every node fetch from every host in parallel, which shortens the crawl","Hostnames are already canonical, so URLs need no normalization before dedupe"],"answer":1,"explain":"Politeness is per host: at most one connection and a delay between requests. If a host's URLs were spread across nodes, those nodes would need to coordinate every fetch. Hostname partitioning is actually less even than URL hashing, since big sites make some nodes heavier, and that is a cost accepted for this benefit.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A crawler must sustain 2,000 pages/s and an average fetch takes 1.5 s including slow servers. About how many fetches must be in flight at once?","options":["About 1,300","About 2,000","About 3,000","About 30,000"],"answer":2,"explain":"Little's Law: in-flight requests = arrival rate × time in system = 2,000/s × 1.5 s = 3,000. That is why fetchers use asynchronous I/O rather than one thread per connection.","level":"easy"},{"q":"The crawler keeps fetching tens of thousands of distinct pages from one site's event calendar, each linking to the next month. The URL-seen set never fires. What is the most robust defense?","options":["Replace the URL-seen set with a Bloom filter so it can track more URLs","Lower the politeness delay for that host so its crawl finishes sooner","Store a content fingerprint of each page and skip exact duplicates","Enforce a per-host page budget per cycle, plus URL length limits"],"answer":3,"explain":"Every calendar URL really is new, and each month's page has different content, so neither URL dedupe nor exact content hashing stops the trap. A per-host budget (backed by URL length and repeated-path limits) bounds the damage from any trap, including ones no heuristic recognizes. Crawling faster only fills the frontier faster.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Two copies of a news article live on different domains and differ only in ad blocks, navigation and a timestamp. Which technique flags them as duplicates?","options":["SimHash over the extracted text, compared by Hamming distance","A SHA-256 hash of the raw HTML response, compared for equality","Normalizing both URLs with canonical rules and comparing them","Comparing the HTTP ETag and Last-Modified headers of both"],"answer":0,"explain":"Raw HTML hashes differ as soon as one byte differs, and the URLs and ETags of different sites are unrelated. SimHash maps similar documents to fingerprints only a few bits apart, which is how Google detected near-duplicates at web scale, and hashing extracted text first removes boilerplate noise.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Why does an autocomplete trie store the top K completions at every node instead of searching the subtree at request time?","options":["It shrinks the trie's memory footprint, since leaf nodes can be dropped","Lookup cost depends on prefix length, not on how many queries share it","It allows the trie to be updated in place on every search without locks","It removes the need to rank suggestions by popularity at build time"],"answer":1,"explain":"The subtree under a short prefix like “s” can hold millions of queries, so searching it per keystroke is too slow. Precomputed lists make a lookup p hops plus a copy. The distractors have it backwards: the lists add memory, and one score change touches every ancestor, which is why the trie is built offline.","level":"medium"},{"q":"The autocomplete index outgrows one machine and must be sharded. Why is hashing each complete query to a shard a poor choice?","options":["Common hash functions mishandle multi-byte Unicode query strings","It concentrates the most popular queries on a single hot shard","A prefix's completions scatter, so every lookup must hit every shard","Hashing makes the daily offline rebuild impossible to parallelize"],"answer":2,"explain":"A prefix lookup needs all completions sharing that prefix, and query hashing spreads them evenly over every shard, turning each keystroke into a scatter-gather. Sharding by prefix range keeps a prefix's subtree on one shard; set range boundaries from traffic histograms to avoid the skew that first-letter sharding causes.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Autocomplete suggestions come from a trie rebuilt nightly, but a breaking news query must start appearing within minutes. What is the best fix?","options":["A streaming job feeds spiking queries into a small overlay merged at read time","Rebuild the entire trie from the query logs and redeploy it every five minutes","Insert each query into the live trie and recompute ancestor top-K under a lock","Disable CDN and browser caching so clients always see the newest suggestions"],"answer":0,"explain":"A small trending overlay, fed by windowed counts in Flink, keeps the main trie immutable and lock-free while adding fresh results. Rebuilding the full index every few minutes is wasteful, and live updates under locks hurt the read path. Disabling caching only adds load: the trie itself would still be stale.","level":"medium"}],"case-studies-social":[{"q":"In a group chat, two members send a message at almost the same instant from phones whose clocks differ by 40 seconds. How should the system guarantee that every member sees the same order?","options":["Sort by the timestamp each phone attaches when the user taps send","Have each gateway deliver messages in the order it received them","Have the conversation's owner server assign a sequence number","Publish to Kafka with a random partition key and order by offset"],"answer":2,"explain":"One owner per conversation assigns a dense, increasing \u003ccode>seq\u003c/code>, so there is exactly one order and clients can detect gaps. Client clocks drift, gateways see different arrival orders, and offsets from different partitions are not comparable, which is why the Kafka key must be the conv_id.","level":"medium"},{"q":"When should a chat client show the sender's first tick ('sent')?","options":["After the server has durably written the message to the message store","As soon as the gateway receives the frame over the WebSocket","When the push notification to the recipient has been handed to APNs or FCM","When the recipient's device acknowledges receiving the message"],"answer":0,"explain":"The first tick is the server's promise that the message will not be lost, so it must follow a durable (quorum) write. Acking at the gateway can lose messages if the next hop fails; the recipient device's ack is what drives the second tick, 'delivered'.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A 500-member group shows 'read by N' for each message. What is the most efficient way to store read state?","options":["A row per (message, member), written when that member reads it","A Redis set of reader IDs per message, expired with a 30-day TTL","A read counter per message, incremented once by every reader","A last-read seq cursor per (conversation, member), moved on read"],"answer":3,"explain":"Reading is monotonic: 'read up to 1044' implies everything before it is read, so one cursor write per read action covers any number of messages. Per-message rows or sets multiply writes by group size, and a bare counter cannot say who read or be safely retried.","level":"medium"},{"q":"You need online and last-seen indicators for 200M concurrent users. Which design scales?","options":["Mark a user offline the instant their TCP socket closes and broadcast it to all contacts","Heartbeats refresh a TTL key per user, and clients subscribe only to users currently on screen","Write every connect and disconnect to the users table and have clients poll it every 5 seconds","Each gateway broadcasts its full list of connected users to every other gateway"],"answer":1,"explain":"TTL keys tolerate flaky mobile networks (missing a couple of heartbeats marks offline, so no flapping), and subscribe-on-view keeps fan-out proportional to what users actually look at. Broadcasting every change to all contacts or polling the database turns presence into the system's largest workload.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Why can't a chat app rely on APNs or FCM to deliver the messages themselves to offline users?","options":["Push services work only while the app holds an open WebSocket connection","Push services require the provider to end-to-end encrypt every payload","Push is best-effort: payloads are small and can be delayed or collapsed","Push services deliver only to the most recently registered device of a user"],"answer":2,"explain":"Push is a wake-up hint. Payloads are capped at 4 KB and providers may delay notifications or keep only the latest one for an offline device, so the app must fetch the actual messages through the socket or a sync call against the server-side log.","level":"easy"},{"q":"In a fan-out-on-write news feed, an account with 50M followers posts and timelines across the fleet lag for minutes. What is the standard fix?","options":["Skip fan-out for huge accounts and merge their posts in at read time","Add fan-out workers until all 50M timeline inserts finish within a second","Switch every user to fan-out on read so that no writes are amplified","Fan out the full post body so that feed reads can skip hydration"],"answer":0,"explain":"The hybrid model pushes for normal authors and pulls for the few celebrities a reader follows, bounding both write amplification and read fan-in. Pure fan-out on read makes every feed load expensive for everyone, and copying bodies makes the write cost worse.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Users of a ranked home feed report duplicates and missing posts while scrolling, because scores change between page requests. What fixes this?","options":["Switch to offset pagination with a smaller, fixed page size","Use a keyset cursor on the ranking score of the last item shown","Re-rank on every page request and let the client drop duplicates","Rank once per session and page through the cached ID list"],"answer":3,"explain":"Freezing the ranking per session makes pages consistent slices of one list; pull-to-refresh starts a new session. Offsets shift as new posts arrive, a cursor on a moving score is not stable, and client-side dedupe cannot recover posts that slipped between pages.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Your Redis home-timeline cluster is running out of memory. Which change saves the most without hurting feed latency for active users?","options":["Store gzip-compressed post JSON in each timeline instead of raw JSON","Store only post IDs, cap each list, and drop inactive users' timelines","Move home timelines to PostgreSQL and query them on each feed load","Switch all users to fan-out on read so no home timelines are stored"],"answer":1,"explain":"IDs are ~8 bytes against ~1 KB bodies, few users scroll past a few hundred items (so a cap around 800 is plenty), and inactive users' timelines can be rebuilt on return. Compression still stores every body, so it saves far less. Moving to a database or to pure pull saves memory but makes every active user's feed slower.","level":"medium"},{"q":"During a 100M-user marketing campaign, password-reset codes take 20 minutes to arrive. What is the most direct architectural fix?","options":["Scale out the notification API so it accepts requests faster","Send all notifications by SMS, which is delivered faster than email","Give critical messages their own priority queue and worker pool","Lower the retry backoff so failed messages are retried sooner"],"answer":2,"explain":"This is a bulkhead problem: the codes are stuck behind campaign jobs in a shared queue. A dedicated high-priority topic and workers, plus pacing campaigns with a token bucket, isolates them. More API instances only accept requests faster; they do not reorder the queue.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A push worker crashes after APNs returned 200 but before it recorded the result, and Kafka redelivers the job. What is the best achievable outcome?","options":["No duplicate, because Kafka exactly-once transactions cover the APNs call","A rare duplicate, minimized by a dedupe key and a collapse ID","No duplicate, because committing the offset before calling APNs is safe","No duplicate, because the worker runs a two-phase commit with APNs"],"answer":1,"explain":"An external HTTP call cannot join a Kafka transaction or a 2PC, so at-least-once plus dedupe is the ceiling, and a collapse ID makes a duplicate replace the earlier notification on the device. Committing the offset first gives at-most-once: a crash then loses the notification instead of duplicating it.","level":"hard"},{"q":"APNs returns \u003ccode>410 Unregistered\u003c/code> for a device token. What should the push worker do?","options":["Disable the token and stop sending to it; do not retry","Retry with exponential backoff up to five times","Send the same notification by SMS immediately","Move the job to the dead-letter queue and replay it tomorrow"],"answer":0,"explain":"410 is a permanent error: the app was uninstalled or the token is no longer active, and Apple expects providers to stop sending to it. Retrying only wastes capacity; transient errors such as 429 or 5xx are the ones to retry. Falling back to SMS is a product decision, not an error handler.","level":"medium"},{"q":"In a 10-person mesh (peer-to-peer) video call with 1.5 Mbps per video stream, roughly how much uplink does each participant need?","options":["1.5 Mbps","13.5 Mbps","15 Mbps","135 Mbps"],"answer":1,"explain":"In a mesh each participant sends a separate copy to each of the other 9 peers: 9 x 1.5 = 13.5 Mbps, beyond many home uplinks, and each copy is encoded separately. With an SFU the uplink is one stream (or one simulcast set of ~2.2 Mbps) regardless of call size.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Users on a corporate network that blocks all outbound UDP cannot join calls. What fixes it?","options":["Deploy more STUN servers inside that region","Increase the receiver's jitter buffer to 500 ms","Switch that meeting from an SFU to an MCU","Offer a TURN relay over TLS on TCP port 443"],"answer":3,"explain":"STUN only discovers a public address over UDP; it cannot carry media through a firewall. TURN over TLS on 443 looks like ordinary HTTPS traffic and relays the media, at the cost of extra latency and relay bandwidth; ICE-TCP directly to the SFU is another option. Changing the topology or buffering does not open a blocked path.","level":"medium"},{"q":"In a 12-person SFU call, one participant is on a 1 Mbps hotel connection. What lets them stay in the call without lowering video quality for everyone else?","options":["Simulcast, with the SFU forwarding low layers to that receiver","The SFU decodes and re-encodes every stream at a lower bitrate for that receiver","Every sender caps its bitrate to what the slowest receiver can handle","Switch media to TCP so lost packets are retransmitted instead of dropped"],"answer":0,"explain":"Simulcast (or SVC) lets the SFU choose a layer per receiver, so the weak participant gets the 180p layers while others keep 720p. Transcoding per receiver works but turns the SFU into a CPU-heavy MCU; capping all senders is what happens without simulcast; TCP adds stalls under loss.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Which requirement most favors an MCU over an SFU?","options":["A 50-person all-hands that requires end-to-end encryption","A 1:1 call between two modern browsers on good networks","Bridging phone dial-in and legacy SIP room systems into the call","A 20-person stand-up where everyone uses the latest desktop app"],"answer":2,"explain":"An MCU decodes, mixes and re-encodes into a single stream, which is what telephony and old SIP or H.323 room systems need. It cannot work with end-to-end encryption because it must decode media; a 1:1 call is cheapest peer to peer, and modern clients in a stand-up are the SFU sweet spot.","level":"hard"}],"case-studies-media":[{"q":"In HLS or DASH playback, what decides which bitrate rendition is fetched for the next segment?","options":["The CDN edge, based on its current load and what it has cached","The player, from its measured throughput and current buffer level","The origin, based on the device type in the User-Agent header","The transcoder, which writes one rendition per viewer at encode time"],"answer":1,"explain":"ABR is client-driven: the manifest lists every rendition, and the player picks one per segment from recent download throughput and buffer level. That keeps servers and CDNs stateless and lets the player react to network changes within one segment.","level":"easy"},{"q":"You expect 14 million concurrent streams at an average bitrate of 3 Mbps. Roughly what egress must the delivery layer sustain on average?","options":["About 4.2 Tbps","About 420 Gbps","About 42 Tbps","About 420 Tbps"],"answer":2,"explain":"14 × 10^6 × 3 × 10^6 bit/s = 4.2 × 10^13 bit/s = 42 Tbps, with evening peaks around twice that. No handful of data centers can egress that much, which is why video delivery is a CDN problem, ideally with caches inside ISPs.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A 2-hour upload takes 6 hours to transcode into 12 renditions on one large machine. Which change cuts time-to-publish the most?","options":["Split the source at keyframes, encode chunk-and-rendition tasks in parallel, then stitch","Move to the largest instance type available and run the 12 renditions one after another","Start transcoding inside the upload request handler so work begins before the upload ends","Encode only the top rendition and have CDN edges downscale it for each viewer on request"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chunks that start on keyframes encode independently, so 720 chunks × 12 renditions become thousands of parallel tasks and wall-clock time approaches the time to encode one chunk. A bigger machine gives only linear gains; transcoding in the request ties a long job to a fragile connection; per-request downscaling at the edge burns enormous compute and defeats caching.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Why must segments in every rendition start on a keyframe at the same timestamps?","options":["So the CDN can dedupe identical segments across different renditions of a title","So one DRM license can be shared across every rendition of the title","So each rendition's media playlist can leave out segment durations","So each segment decodes alone and the player can switch at any boundary"],"answer":3,"explain":"A segment that starts with an IDR keyframe needs no earlier frames to decode, and aligned boundaries mean segment N in 480p covers the same time range as segment N in 1080p, so the player can switch cleanly. Segments in different renditions contain different bytes, so nothing is deduped, and playlists still list segment durations.","level":"hard"},{"q":"A hit series releases its new season at midnight, and millions of viewers will press play within minutes. Which CDN approach protects the origin best?","options":["Pull-through caching with a short TTL, so each edge fetches segments on first request","Pre-position the episodes on ISP-embedded caches in an off-peak fill window","Add origin servers in every region and spread viewers across them with GeoDNS","Raise Cache-Control max-age on the manifests so edges keep them longer once fetched"],"answer":1,"explain":"Demand for a scheduled release is predictable, so push the files to the edge before anyone asks, as Netflix Open Connect does during nightly fill windows. Pull-through caching makes thousands of edges miss at the same moment, and more origin servers don't remove the backbone bottleneck.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A video goes viral at 200k views per minute, and \u003ccode>UPDATE videos SET views = views + 1\u003c/code> on its row starts timing out. What is the standard fix?","options":["Wrap the increment in SELECT FOR UPDATE so concurrent writers queue up in order","Add read replicas so the increments spread across more database servers","Stream watch events to Kafka and apply aggregated deltas per time window","Have each player compute the running total and report it to the server periodically"],"answer":2,"explain":"Stream aggregation turns 200k row updates per minute into one delta per video every few seconds, and it can dedupe and filter bots along the way. Replicas don't spread writes, explicit locking makes contention on a hot row worse, and clients can't be trusted to report totals.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Driver locations arrive at about 500k writes per second, and each one is useless after about 30 seconds. Where should the live positions be kept?","options":["In an in-memory geo index sharded by city, with pings also logged to Kafka","In a PostgreSQL table with a spatial index, updated in place on every ping","In Elasticsearch with a geo_point field, refreshing the index after every update","In object storage, with one object per driver overwritten every four seconds"],"answer":0,"explain":"The live set is only a few hundred MB and changes constantly, so memory is the right home, and a lost ping is replaced 4 s later. Kafka keeps the history for receipts and analytics. A transactional database would spend most of its effort on WAL and index churn for data that expires in seconds.","level":"easy"},{"q":"Why does Uber's H3 index use hexagonal cells rather than squares?","options":["Hexagons tile the entire sphere exactly, with no exceptions at any resolution","Every hexagon nests exactly inside one parent, so containment queries are exact","Hexagon cell IDs need fewer bits than square cell IDs at the same resolution","All six neighbors are equidistant from the center, so k-rings grow evenly"],"answer":3,"explain":"A square has 4 edge neighbors and 4 corner neighbors about 1.4 times farther away; a hexagon's 6 neighbors are equidistant, which keeps ring searches, smoothing and movement modeling uniform. The tempting options are false: H3 needs 12 pentagons at every resolution, and its children only approximately fit inside their parent.","level":"medium"},{"q":"Two dispatcher instances pick the same available driver for two different riders at the same moment. What prevents a double assignment with the least coordination?","options":["Take a city-wide distributed lock in ZooKeeper before running every match","Send both offers and let the driver pick whichever rider they prefer","Atomically flip the driver from AVAILABLE to RESERVED; the loser moves on","Run a two-phase commit across the trip database and the geo index shard"],"answer":2,"explain":"An atomic CAS on the one driver's state, at the shard that owns the driver, serializes only the contention that actually exists. A city-wide lock serializes every match in the city, letting the driver choose leaves the other rider waiting for nothing, and 2PC adds blocking coordination to every match.","level":"hard"},{"q":"An app overwrites 50 KB in the middle of a 2 GB file in place, without changing its length. The file is stored as 4 MB content-hashed blocks. What should the sync client upload?","options":["The whole 2 GB file again, so the server can re-chunk it consistently","Only the blocks the server reports missing from the new hash list","A binary diff that the server computes against the previous revision","Every block from the edited one to the end of the file, since offsets shift"],"answer":1,"explain":"The client re-hashes the file, sends the hash list, and uploads only the one or two blocks whose hashes changed. Nothing after them shifts because the length didn't change. An insertion would shift every later fixed-size boundary, which is the problem content-defined chunking solves.","level":"easy"},{"q":"A teammate proposes committing a new file revision as soon as the metadata service receives the block list, with the blocks uploaded afterwards to cut latency. What breaks?","options":["Other devices can see a revision whose blocks are missing, possibly forever","Dedupe stops working, because the block index is updated before the blocks are hashed","Conflicts can no longer be detected, because parent revisions are not checked","Cursors on other devices can move backwards and replay old journal entries"],"answer":0,"explain":"A revision must only become visible once every block it references is durable. Otherwise other devices are told about a file they can't download, and a crash mid-upload leaves a permanently broken revision. Conflict checks and cursors don't depend on when blocks arrive, which is why the check-upload-commit order exists.","level":"hard"},{"q":"Alice and Bob edit the same file in a shared folder while both are offline. Alice reconnects first. What should a Dropbox-style system do when Bob reconnects?","options":["Keep whichever version has the later modification time on its device clock","Merge the two versions line by line and save the result as the new revision","Reject Bob's commit and discard his local edits so that all devices converge","Reject Bob's stale-parent commit and keep his version as a conflicted copy"],"answer":3,"explain":"The parent-revision compare-and-set detects the conflict, and keeping a conflicted copy guarantees nobody's work disappears. Last-writer-wins on device clocks silently discards an edit and is skewed by clock drift; a line merge corrupts binary formats; discarding Bob's edits loses data.","level":"medium"},{"q":"How should the booking service hold seats while a fan spends up to 10 minutes paying?","options":["Open a transaction with SELECT FOR UPDATE on the seats and commit it after payment","Mark the seats as held in the browser's local storage and validate them at checkout","Conditionally UPDATE the seats to HELD, with an expiry after which they are free","Decrement a cached counter and write the seat assignments to the database after payment"],"answer":2,"explain":"A hold is state, not a lock: one short conditional write marks the seats HELD with an expiry, and any later attempt can reclaim them once the expiry passes. A transaction held open across human think time ties up locks and connections for minutes; client-side state and deferred writes cannot prevent double booking.","level":"medium"},{"q":"A payment webhook arrives after the fan's hold expired and the seats were sold to someone else. What is the correct behavior?","options":["Confirm the order anyway and let the venue sort out the duplicate seat at the gate","Confirmation updates zero rows, so refund automatically and notify the fan","Cancel the second fan's booking, because the first fan started checking out earlier","Retry the confirmation with backoff until the other fan's booking is released"],"answer":1,"explain":"Confirmation moves seats to BOOKED only where the hold still owns them, so a lost hold shows up as zero rows updated and triggers an idempotent refund. The second fan's booking is legitimate. Expiring the payment session a minute or two before the hold makes this race rare.","level":"hard"},{"q":"In a virtual waiting room, why give a random position to everyone who arrived before the sale opened instead of serving them strictly first come, first served?","options":["Strict FIFO rewards whoever fires at 10:00:00, which favors bots and scripts","Random positions spread fans evenly across the database shards that hold the show","Random positions are what make the now-serving counter cacheable at the CDN edge","Strict FIFO needs a distributed lock, while random positions need no coordination"],"answer":0,"explain":"Randomizing everyone who arrived early removes the race at 10:00:00.000 that scripts always win, so arriving early is enough and refreshing gains nothing. The queue is still two counters either way: it has nothing to do with shards, and the now-serving value is cacheable regardless of how positions are assigned.","level":"medium"}]};